{"raw": {"https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057250434": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Physics", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Physics. Job category: Physics, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Carroll University seeks to hire Adjunct Instructors of Physics for Spring 2027 Please indicate the course(s) and section(s) you are interested and available to teach in the application's additional comments field and/or your cover letter. PHY 101L | Introductory Physics I Laboratory Course: Lab component for PHY101, t he first course of a non-calculus based two-course sequence in the basic principles of physics covering the areas of mechanics, thermal physics, and fluids. Section C: 12:00 p.m. - 1:50 p.m. | Tuesdays | On Campus Term: Spring 2027 (January 19 - May 4) Qualifications: A degree in Physics or related field. Graduate degree preferred (M.S. or Ph.D.). Official transcripts due upon hire. PHY 102L | Introductory Physics II Laboratory Course: Lab component for PHY102, the second course of a non-calculus based two-course sequence in the basic principles of physics including wave motion (oscillations, waves, and sound), light and optics, and electromagnetism. Section C: 12:00 p.m. - 1:50 p.m. | Thursdays | On Campus Term: Spring 2027 (January 19 - May 4) Qualifications: A degree in Physics or related field. Graduate degree preferred (M.S. or Ph.D.). Official transcripts due upon hire. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments - Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. In your cover letter or in the comments, please indicate the course number/section and name to which you are applying. Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057250433": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Mathematics", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Mathematics. Job category: Mathematics, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Carroll University seeks to hire Adjunct Instructor of Mathematics/Computational Science Please indicate the course(s) and section(s) you are interested and available to teach in the application's additional comments field and/or your cover letter. CMP 112 | Computational Thinking I Course: The course introduces students to elementary statistical methods and intermediate-level uses of Excel spreadsheets with a focus on data analysis, organization, visualization, and interpretation. Students will be exposed to some essential elements of computational thinking. They will solve problems with the statistical and software tools they've learned by developing simple algorithms and using elementary statistical models. The topics may include, but are not limited to, descriptive statistics, 1- and 2-parameter tests (e.g. 2 independent sample t-tests) as well as correlation and regression. Probabilistic models will be used to simulate stochastic processes and develop concepts in algorithmic thinking and mathematical modeling. Section K: 9:20 a.m. - 10:30 a.m. | Mondays, Wednesdays, Fridays | On Campus Term: Spring 2027 (January 19 - May 4) Qualifications : A graduate degree (M.S. or Ph.D.) in Mathematics or related field. Official transcripts due upon hire. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments - Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. In your cover letter or in the comments, please indicate the course number/section and name to which you are applying. Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057240201": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Education", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Education. Job category: Education, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct instructor for EDU235"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057236167": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Animal Behavior", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Animal Behavior. Job category: Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Position Overview The Local Puppy Counselor supports Carroll University student puppy raisers participating in the Leader Dogs for the Blind puppy raising program. This position provides training, guidance, and individualized support to student raisers while serving as the primary liaison between students and LDFB. The Puppy Counselor also coordinates training opportunities, monitors puppy progress, facilitates communication, and supports the broader campus puppy raising community. Primary Responsibilities 1. Puppy Raiser Training and Development Design, coordinate, and lead a minimum of one in-person puppy training outing per month, as required by LDFB. Typically offer two monthly training opportunities\u2014one on-campus and one off-campus\u2014to provide additional training and support for student raisers. Provide individualized training and behavioral support to student raisers as needed or upon request, including phone calls, text messages, emails, and private training sessions. Submit required Puppy Seen reports to LDFB following each training outing. 2. Communication and Program Coordination Serve as the primary liaison between LDFB and Carroll University student puppy raisers and any CU faculty providing general oversight to the program. Facilitate communication between CU and LDFB regarding puppy behavioral and medical concerns, training progress, and requests for supplies or equipment. Develop and distribute newsletters to student puppy raisers containing training guidance, program updates, and reminders about LDFB requirements. Develop and distribute monthly surveys to collect puppy progress reports and identify training or support needs. Attend annual Puppy Counselor training at LDFB in Michigan. 3. Puppy Sitter Training and Evaluation Design and conduct a minimum of one puppy sitter training session annually for Carroll University students, faculty, or staff or public citizens who are not puppy raisers, with a goal of offering one session per semester. Coordinate and conduct interviews with prospective student puppy sitters. Evaluate prospective puppy sitters through practical skills checks to assess their readiness to care for puppies in the program. 4. Student Organization Support Provide reasonable assistance and guidance to Pio Paws, Carroll University's student organization supporting puppy raising, as needed or upon request. Support student-led activities and initiatives that contribute to the success of the puppy raising program while maintaining student responsibility for organization planning and operations. 5. Internship Supervision and Evaluation Complete and submit supervisor feedback forms to Carroll University staff for student puppy raisers who participate in the program as part of their internship requirements. Provide feedback regarding student performance and progress as required by the internship program. Position Expectations The Local Puppy Counselor is expected to maintain professional, supportive relationships with student raisers, LDFB personnel, and Carroll University staff. Responsibilities include regularly scheduled training and administrative activities, as well as individualized support in response to student and puppy needs. The position requires independent planning, communication, documentation, and coordination to ensure that student raisers receive appropriate guidance and meet LDFB program expectations."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057235456": {"title": "Assistant Director of Alumni Reunions and Awards", "description": "Assistant Director of Alumni Reunions and Awards. Job category: Advancement, Alumni Engagement Employee type: Administrative Staff. At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Assistant Director of Alumni Reunions and Awards is responsible for assisting in the creation, development, implementation, management and evaluation of alumni programs and events designed to engage alumni and foster relationships on behalf of Carroll University. Responsibilities: Plan and implement an annual calendar of programs and activities which develop strong institutional affiliation with Carroll University alumni. This includes, but is not limited to, Homecoming, Reunion & Family Weekend, the Distinguished Alumni Awards Event, on-campus and local events and regional events. Serve as the lead for the coordination and management of Homecoming, Reunion & Family Weekend and Alumni Class Reunions, collaborating with campus partners, external vendors and other alumni engagement and development team colleagues. Support and coordinate a strong annual reunion program, in collaboration with other alumni engagement team members and development colleagues, utilizing alumni volunteers to build participation for their milestone reunions and strengthen university affiliation. Coordinate and execute the Distinguished Alumni Award (DAA) process and event in partnership with the Distinguished Alumni Award Committee of the Alumni Council. This includes but is not limited to soliciting award nominations, facilitating the selection process, communication with DAA applicants and selected recipients and management and execution of the event. Assist the Executive Director in supporting the Alumni Council, including attending meetings and serving as the lead staff resource for the Distinguished Alumni Award Committee and other provisional committees as needed. Coordinate, plan and staff regional alumni events as assigned. Build alumni events in our system, utilizing various platforms. Assist in creating, editing and managing all alumni email communications. Assist the alumni team with regularly editing email communications, the online alumni monthly newsletter and all other alumni communications including but not limited to: invitations, letters, F1RST magazine, etc. Assist in managing the alumni email inbox as needed and respond to alumni inquiries accordingly. Also, assist the alumni team with managing alumni office phone calls. Create, with the alumni engagement team, an annual strategic operating plan which includes established measurable goals, a calendar of events, communication and messaging plan to alumni and an anticipated budget. Attend and assist in the facilitation of all alumni events as needed. Nights and weekends will be required. Travel as needed and provide appropriate support to associated programs and events. Assist the Executive Director and other Advancement staff with activities as they specifically relate to the cultivation and stewardship of alumni. Perform other duties as requested, delegated or assigned. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: Bachelor's degree required. Three to five years of related experience in a similar position for a non-profit organization, preferably in higher education. Event planning experience required. Strong written, interpersonal, and oral communication skills required. Availability for frequent evening and weekend work required. Preference will be given to experience in creating and developing thoughtful alumni programs and activities and experience in coordinating volunteers. Knowledge of Microsoft Windows, Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Outlook. Experience in Blackbaud programs such as Raiser\u2019s Edge and Net Community are a plus. Additional Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Ability to clearly articulate, support and advance the mission of Carroll University. Strong presentation skills and comfort with public speaking. Strong interpersonal and organizational skills and an aptitude for working with people of different ages and temperaments. Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background or job type. Building meaningful relationships with constituents to build strong alumni engagement and support is critical to this role. Requires the ability to analyze situations and implement appropriate actions and responses. Strong writing skills in order to develop professional reports, strategic plans and correspondence are necessary. High energy level, self-motivated, and self-directed to design and complete complex projects. Must also have the skills and desire to: multi-task and meet deadlines; be a team player; work collaboratively; maintain strong organizational skills; understand the principles and techniques relevant to fundraising; participation in evening and weekend activities are necessary. Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 N. East Ave, Waukesha, WI 53186."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040514249": {"title": "Part-Time Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant Studies (.5 FTE)", "description": "Part-Time Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant Studies (.5 FTE). Job category: Physician Assistant, Faculty Employee type: Regular Faculty. Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant Studies (Part-time Position-Didactic) Starting Date: May 1, 2027 Carroll University seeks a part-time Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant Studies for its innovative Master of Science in Physician Assistant Studies (MSPAS) program. In addition to traditional Physician Assistant studies, the program is dedicated to developing students' competency to serve medically underserved populations and facilitate interprofessional healthcare practice. Candidates who are innovative and value a dynamic and comprehensive approach to curriculum, instruction, and physician assistant practice are encouraged to apply. This is a .5 FTE, 12-month position. Qualifications: Carroll University (www.carrollu.edu) invites applications for the position of Clinical Assistant Professor of the Master of Physician Assistant Studies Program. Candidates will: \u00b7 Be an NCCPA board-certified physician assistant \u00b7 Hold a terminal degree in physician assistant studies (MSPAS or MPAS) \u00b7 Hold current physician assistant licensure or be eligible for licensure \u00b7 Possess a minimum of 5 years of clinical experience Responsibilities : This principal faculty will report to the Program Director and be responsible for and actively participate in the processes that include: Developing, reviewing and revising as necessary the mission statement, goals, and competencies of the program Select applicants for admission to the PA program, including recruitment, application review, interviewing, and selection Providing student instruction in the didactic year Evaluate student performance in both didactic and clinical year assessments Provide academic counseling/advising to assigned students Assure the availability of remedial instruction Service to the PA Program and University, including participation in the PA Program (Curriculum) Committee and the annual retreat Data collection and analysis for the program\u2019s annual assessment report Service and professional activity may include, but is not limited to, programmatic projects, academic advising, admissions work, and clinical practice within our program, including the Waukesha Free Clinic at Carroll University, that involves acting as a preceptor to our students. Rank and Salary: This is a clinical track position at the rank of assistant professor. Salary is commensurate with experience. The Program: The Physician Assistant Studies Program educates physician assistants to provide comprehensive quality health care to all, respectful of patient values, committed to ethical principles, and grounded in evidence-based practice and clinical reasoning. Graduates contribute to their profession and communities and are prepared to practice medicine in a variety of primary care settings under the supervision of physicians. Graduates are prepared to provide service and practice in medically underserved communities and diverse patient populations. Carroll University partners with and houses the independent Waukesha Free Clinic and serves as one of the required clinical rotations for program students. The MSPAS program is accredited by the Accreditation Review Commission on Education for the Physician Assistant (ARC-PA), providing education to a 40 student cohort. The program has a strong commitment to inter-disciplinary team building, and faculty include PA-Cs, PhDs, and MDs. The program is located in the new 61,000 sq. ft. College of Health Sciences interdisciplinary graduate teaching and research facility, with teaching and laboratory space specifically designed for the MSPAS program. In addition, the program utilizes the Michael and Mary Jaharis Laboratories for its cadaver lab and state-of-the-art interdisciplinary simulation laboratories. The College: The Von Hoff College of Health Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments\u2014 Athletic Training, Human Movement Sciences, Interprofessional Studies, Nursing, Occupational Therapy, Physical Therapy, and Physician Assistant Studies. Health sciences account for ~60% of the University enrollment. The mission of the College of Health Science is to educate students to be innovative professionals who collaborate to serve the health needs of their communities. A dynamic and growing entity, College wide foci include interprofessional education and practice, the integrated approach to health, innovative learning and healthcare technologies, community partnerships and service to medically underserved populations. The new interdisciplinary, 61,000 sq. ft., College of Health Science building, opening in the fall of 2026, provides state of the art teaching, simulation, and patient treatment spaces. This building houses our graduate Exercise Physiology, Occupational Therapy, Pathologist Assistant, and Physician Assistant programs. While the 55,000 sq. ft. Center for Graduate Studies houses our graduate Athletic Training and Doctor of Physical Therapy Programs. The Carroll University Community Health Services building expands education opportunities, while providing valuable pro bono services for the local community. The College supports the interdisciplinary nature of health science education and has received federal and private foundation grant awards to support these as well as program specific initiatives. The University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive University. The Carroll mission is to \u201cprovide a superior education, rooted in its Presbyterian and liberal arts heritage, and draws upon the Christian tradition to prepare all students for vocational success, lifelong learning and service in a diverse and global society.\u201d Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves 3600 undergraduate and graduate students. The University though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057227905": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Environmental Science", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Environmental Science. Job category: Environmental Science, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Carroll University seeks to hire Adjunct Instructors of Environmental Science Please indicate the course(s) and section(s) you are interested and available to teach in the application's additional comments field and/or your cover letter. ENV 120 | Conservation and Environmental Improvement Course: A study of global natural resources and methods used in conservation. Includes the basic concepts of ecological biology including interactions between the living and the non-living elements of the environment, concepts of energy transformation in physical and biological systems, the nature of the Earth's ecosystems, and the implications of continued growth of the human population. Emphasis is placed on human environmental concerns and methods to be used to study and alleviate human environmental problems. Section A: 8:00 a.m. - 9:50 a.m. | Tuesdays, Thursdays | On Campus Term: Spring 2027 (January 19 - May 4). Qualifications: A graduate degree in Environmental Science (M.S. or Ph.D.) or related field. Official transcripts due upon hire. ENV 120L | Conservation and Environmental Improvement Laboratory Course: Lab component for ENV120, a study of global natural resources and methods used in conservation. Section A: 10:15 a.m. - 1:05 p.m. | Thursdays | On Campus Section B: 1:20 p.m. - 4:10 p.m. | Thursdays | On Campus Term: Spring 2027 (January 19 - May 4). Qualifications: A degree in Environmental Science or related field. Graduate degree (M.S. or Ph.D.) preferred. Official transcripts due upon hire. ENV 277 | Natural Resource Management Course: The course provides an introduction to the biological and social aspects of renewable and nonrenewable natural resource management. In addition to the management of fisheries and wildlife populations and their habitats, emphasis will be placed on forestry, vegetative communities, and non-renewable (fossil fuel) natural resources. Section A: 8:00 a.m. - 9:50 a.m. | Tuesdays, Thursdays | On Campus Term: Spring 2027 (January 19 - May 4). Qualifications: A graduate degree in Environmental Science (M.S. or Ph.D.) or related field. Official transcripts due upon hire. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments - Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. In your cover letter or in the comments, please indicate the course number/section and name to which you are a pplying. Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057227328": {"title": "Custodian", "description": "Custodian. Job category: Physical Plant Employee type: Support Staff. Responsibilities: Demonstrate proper and effective cleaning methods in accordance with the quality control checklist Clean, sweep, vacuum, mop, scrub and wax floors, stairs and other surfaces Clean and service lavatories and shower room facilities Use light and heavy duty cleaning equipment and attachments Report items and equipment that needs repair (doors, furniture, lights, and faucets, etc.) Follow and comply with all safety and work rules and regulations, opens and closes, locks and unlocks facilities as needed Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Educational and/or Experience High school graduate (diploma/GED) preferred Knowledge of proper use and disposal of hazardous chemicals and products Knowledge of bodily fluid cleanup and proper protection Knowledge of all floor types, such as wood, vinyl and ceramic tile as well as carpet fibers, weaves and backings Three years of experience in a related field preferred Human Relations Skills Ability to be flexible and prioritize work in a consistently changing environment. Establish and maintain working relationships with employees, staff, and faculty and campus visitors. Follow oral and/or written instructions. Communicate effectively in both oral and written form. Ability to work as part of a team and independently as required. Physical Demands Considerable physical activity is required. Requires heavy physical work; heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling required of objects up to 50 pounds. Physical work is primary part (more than 70%) of job. Work environment involves some exposure to hazards or physical risks, which require following basic safety precautions. Work may involve moderate exposure to unusual elements, such as extreme temperatures, dirt, dust, fumes, smoke, unpleasant odors, and/or loud noises. Work in all types of weather and temperatures both indoors (temperature variation in campus buildings) and outdoors (precipitation and various temperatures). Frequent and repetitive bending, twisting, reaching, pushing, and pulling are required. Use proper lifting (up to 15 pounds) and safety techniques while climbing ladders. Other Requirements Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs Valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University's insurance plan preferred Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission ."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040508997": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Music", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Music. Job category: Music, Adjunct, Music Technology Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Music for Spring 2027 Carroll University seeks to hire an Adjunct Instructor of Music for Spring 2027. Course: MUS133 | Music Technology (1 Credit) Section A: Friday, 8:00 \u2013 9:10 AM Ever wondered how to get that perfect live sound or create professional-quality recordings? This hands-on course will transform you from tech-timid to tech-savvy! Designed specifically for music students, this course bridges the gap between your musical expertise and the technical skills demanded by today\u2019s music industry. Through practical, real-world scenarios, you\u2019ll learn essential skills that every modern musician needs. From setting up sound systems for live performances to creating polished recordings, this course empowers you to take control of your sound. Qualifications: Graduate degree in discipline. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments \u2013 Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. Please indicate the course section letter(s) to which you are applying within your cover letter. Carroll University makes employment opportunities available to qualified individuals whose skills and experience make them suitable candidates for placement. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Carroll University does not discriminate in any manner contrary to law or justice on the basis of race, color, sex, age, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, disability or veteran's status in administration of programs or in the employment of faculty and staff. The employment opportunities list is updated periodically based on openings. Applications will be accepted only for vacancies currently listed. All inquiries will be kept strictly confidential. Graduate Transcript(s) \u2013 Unofficial graduate transcripts will suffice for initial application. However, official graduate transcripts will be required for all final faculty candidates/new hires. To be considered \u201cOfficial,\u201d transcripts must be sent directly from the issuing institution to Carroll University. If you wish to submit official transcripts at time of initial application, please request they be sent electronically to cuaa@carrollu.edu or be sent as a sealed hard copy to the address provided below: Carroll University Attn: Academic Affairs - Voorhees 232 100 N. East Avenue Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040507394": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Sociology", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Sociology. Job category: Sociology, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Sociology for Spring 2027 Carroll University seeks to hire an Adjunct Instructor of Sociology for Spring 2027. Course: SOC105 | Introduction to Social Work Section A: Tuesday and Thursday, 2:00 \u2013 3:50 PM This course examines the profession of social work and the role it plays in the social welfare system. Students will investigate the history of the profession, the social welfare policies that form the basis of the field, and the many types of situations in which social workers work. Qualifications: Graduate degree in discipline. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments \u2013 Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. Please indicate the course section letter(s) to which you are applying within your cover letter. Carroll University makes employment opportunities available to qualified individuals whose skills and experience make them suitable candidates for placement. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Carroll University does not discriminate in any manner contrary to law or justice on the basis of race, color, sex, age, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, disability or veteran's status in administration of programs or in the employment of faculty and staff. The employment opportunities list is updated periodically based on openings. Applications will be accepted only for vacancies currently listed. All inquiries will be kept strictly confidential. Graduate Transcript(s) \u2013 Unofficial graduate transcripts will suffice for initial application. However, official graduate transcripts will be required for all final faculty candidates/new hires. To be considered \u201cOfficial,\u201d transcripts must be sent directly from the issuing institution to Carroll University. If you wish to submit official transcripts at time of initial application, please request they be sent electronically to cuaa@carrollu.edu or be sent as a sealed hard copy to the address provided below: Carroll University Attn: Academic Affairs - Voorhees 232 100 N. East Avenue Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040507393": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Communication", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Communication. Job category: Communication, Adjunct, Media Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Communication for Spring 2027 Carroll University seeks to hire an Adjunct Instructor of Communication for Spring 2027. Course: COM112 | Media Lab: Career Exploration in the Media Industry (2 Credits) Section A: Monday, 6:00 \u2013 7:50 PM In this hybrid course, students explore the variety of pathways leading to a rewarding career in the media field. Facilitated by media professionals, the course introduces students to the professional competencies central to careers in print journalism, radio and television broadcasting, podcasting, social media, advertising, and public relations. Qualifications: Graduate degree in discipline. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments \u2013 Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. Please indicate the course section letter(s) to which you are applying within your cover letter. Carroll University makes employment opportunities available to qualified individuals whose skills and experience make them suitable candidates for placement. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Carroll University does not discriminate in any manner contrary to law or justice on the basis of race, color, sex, age, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, disability or veteran's status in administration of programs or in the employment of faculty and staff. The employment opportunities list is updated periodically based on openings. Applications will be accepted only for vacancies currently listed. All inquiries will be kept strictly confidential. Graduate Transcript(s) \u2013 Unofficial graduate transcripts will suffice for initial application. However, official graduate transcripts will be required for all final faculty candidates/new hires. To be considered \u201cOfficial,\u201d transcripts must be sent directly from the issuing institution to Carroll University. If you wish to submit official transcripts at time of initial application, please request they be sent electronically to cuaa@carrollu.edu or be sent as a sealed hard copy to the address provided below: Carroll University Attn: Academic Affairs - Voorhees 232 100 N. East Avenue Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040506054": {"title": "Building Maintenance Technician II", "description": "Building Maintenance Technician II. Job category: Physical Plant - Maintenance Employee type: Support Staff. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Building Maintenance Technician II provides a skillset within the trades field. The building maintenance technician team provides support for a variety of campus trades needs. Performs a variety of skilled maintenance for campus facilities; ensures that facilities are available for use and consistently maintained in a safe and clean condition. Areas of responsibilities include, but are not limited to: o General Carpentry o Painting o Drywall o Plaster o Cabinetry and Furniture o Window Treatments Repair/Replace o Flooring o Appliance repair o Basic Lock and Door Hardware o Basic Plumbing o Basic Electric o Basic HVAC o General Cleaning Practices \u00b7 Demonstrates strong customer service skills and can establish, maintain, and foster positive and effective working relationships. \u00b7 General practices and operational knowledge of tools used in the maintenance and repair of building facilities including upkeep and maintenance of tools. \u00b7 Occupational hazards and standard safety practices. \u00b7 Principles and practices of recordkeeping and facilities maintenance software. \u00b7 Identifies and reports life safety and maintenance needs to the supervisor. \u00b7 Comply with University facility standards of conduct. \u00b7 Accomplishing work orders through our facility maintenance software on a daily basis. \u00b7 Maintain and manage records on fixed asset expenditures \u00b7 Assist with project work scopes for building maintenance projects. Remove snow and ice from stairs, walks and ramps including shoveling, salting and plowing. \u00b7 Perform other duties as assigned by your supervisor. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience \u00b7 High School Diploma/GED required \u00b7 Trade school, apprenticeship, or other formal training preferred \u00b7 3 - 5 years of experience in a specific trade preferred or general maintenance, preference of locksmith experience or the ability to complete required locksmith training within 1 year of hire \u00b7 Working familiarity with standard building codes Human Relations Skills \u00b7 Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with diverse constituencies (internal and external) \u00b7 Strong written and verbal communication skills \u00b7 Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Technology Skills \u00b7 PC proficient; experience with Office 365 preferred \u00b7 Understanding of Word, Excel, and Power Point \u00b7 Working knowledge and proficiency with maintenance management system preferred Physical Demands \u00b7 Ability to stoop, kneel, reach, and inspect work sites. \u00b7 Sufficient strength and dexterity to perform duties and responsibilities of the position \u00b7 Job requires the ability to lift, carry, push, and pull materials and objects weighing up to 100 pounds or heavier weights with assistance and/or the use of proper equipment. Environmental Elements \u00b7 Work in both indoor and outdoor environments with moderate to loud noise levels. \u00b7 May work in controlled temperature conditions, cold and hot temperatures, or inclement weather conditions. \u00b7 Exposure to chemicals, mechanical and electrical hazards, and hazardous physical substances and fumes. Other Requirements \u00b7 Must have valid driver\u2019s license and be insurable under the university\u2019s insurance plan. \u00b7 Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs \u00b7 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University Ethos Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 East Ave., Waukesha, WI 53186."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040507392": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Theatre Arts", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Theatre Arts. Job category: Theatre Arts, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Theatre Arts for Spring 2027 Carroll University seeks to hire an Adjunct Instructor of Theatre Arts for Spring 2027. Course: THE313 | Writing the Short Film (4 Credits) Section A: Tuesday, 6:00 \u2013 9:35 PM A practical, workshop-style course is writing short films, including a study of basic short film history, structures and concepts, as well as how to navigate the short film festival application and submission process. Qualifications: Graduate degree in discipline. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments \u2013 Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. Please indicate the course section letter(s) to which you are applying within your cover letter. Carroll University makes employment opportunities available to qualified individuals whose skills and experience make them suitable candidates for placement. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Carroll University does not discriminate in any manner contrary to law or justice on the basis of race, color, sex, age, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, disability or veteran's status in administration of programs or in the employment of faculty and staff. The employment opportunities list is updated periodically based on openings. Applications will be accepted only for vacancies currently listed. All inquiries will be kept strictly confidential. Graduate Transcript(s) \u2013 Unofficial graduate transcripts will suffice for initial application. However, official graduate transcripts will be required for all final faculty candidates/new hires. To be considered \u201cOfficial,\u201d transcripts must be sent directly from the issuing institution to Carroll University. If you wish to submit official transcripts at time of initial application, please request they be sent electronically to cuaa@carrollu.edu or be sent as a sealed hard copy to the address provided below: Carroll University Attn: Academic Affairs - Voorhees 232 100 N. East Avenue Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040504197": {"title": "Reservations and Event Services Operations Coordinator", "description": "Reservations and Event Services Operations Coordinator. Job category: Reservations and Event Services, Staff Employee type: Support Staff. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Operations Coordinator is responsible for training and supervising a student staff of 25+, as well as coordinating event services for both internal and external clients. The incumbent will collaborate with constituencies across the University to ensure successful coordination and allocation of resources, compliance with policies and procedures, inventory management, scheduling and supervision of student staff. The position offers a flexible work schedule, with the opportunity for overtime. Responsibilities: Event management: Plan and coordinate services required to support events scheduled through the Office of Reservations and Event Services, including services required from vendors, rental equipment suppliers and audio/visual providers; ensure proper on-site event management for all events. Facilitate appropriate communication between clients and other internal service providers; collaborate with campus departments, including the registrar, athletics, dining services, residence life and housing, Office of Information Technology (OIT), facilities, public safety and risk management, to ensure availability and coordination of facilities and delivery of services necessary for successful events. Represent the Office of Reservations and Event Services and assist other departments in the coordination and planning of major campus events Assist the Director in the general operation and management of facilities e.g. maintenance, policies, regulations and event services Provide evening and weekend coverage for scheduled events as required Student staff supervision: Supervise thorough and efficient setup and teardown for all events Serve as primary supervisor and lead with student workers and contracted providers as determined by the Director Recruit, hire, train, schedule, evaluate and perform other required administrative tasks relating to student staff including financial aid tracking Develop and manage schedules for student workers Administrative: Assist with overall management of event scheduling software; process event requests, add/edit user profiles, maintain inventory and ensure client requests are monitored and completed Identify maintenance, custodial, and equipment issues that require attention and submit work orders as needed Maintain equipment inventory; coordinate equipment purchases as needed Assist with departmental marketing strategies, and social media account maintenance Demonstrate professionalism at all times when interacting with clients, faculty/staff, and students Know, understand, and enforce all applicable policies and procedures related to the university and scheduling Identify and participate in ongoing professional development, continuing education and training as required; play an active role in industry associations such as ACCED-I, NACAS, ACUI and/or other appropriate groups. Create diagrams and floor layouts when necessary for campus events Serve as a back-up to other staff members in their absence Perform other duties as assigned in support of department and/or division needs and goals. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: Bachelor\u2019s degree required; Master\u2019s degree preferred Minimum one year of event planning or related experience is required. Operations experience is preferred Higher education work experience with knowledge of best practices is preferred Experience with event scheduling software is preferred Event setup/teardown experience is preferred Technology Skills: Database management Thorough knowledge of MS Office applications (Word, Excel, PowerPoint) Use and maintain voicemail and e-mail Experience with Visio, CAD, and/or other related software is preferred General understanding of client-server computing and networking Technical proficiency with lighting, audio-visual, and related equipment is preferred Human Relations Skills: Attention to detail Exceptional time-management skills Strong communication, management, and administrative skills Ability to maintain confidentiality High customer service skills Ability to work as a team member and to work with a diverse group of constituencies Ability to work evenings, weekends, and holidays as needed Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Physical Demands: Ability to stand for extended periods of time Ability to sit for extended periods of time Ability to walk for extended periods of time Ability to lift and carry 50 pounds or more Special Requirements: Valid drivers\u2019 license and ability to be insured under the University's insurance plan required Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 East Ave., Waukesha, WI 53186."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040503235": {"title": "Executive Assistant to the Provost", "description": "Executive Assistant to the Provost. Job category: Academic Affairs, Staff Employee type: Administrative Staff. Description: The Executive Assistant to the Provost is responsible for the management and support of the Office of Academic Affairs, Faculty Governance Committees and academic initiatives. This individual serves as a first point of contact for the Provost and Vice-President for Academic Affairs and plays a key role in the operations of the office. Responsibilities: Management and support of the Office of Academic Affairs and the Provost by: Serving as first contact to the Provost for questions, requests for information, and concerns of faculty, staff, and student Providing organizational management of the Office of Academic Affairs by scheduling appointments, disseminating meeting agendas, attending meetings and completing follow-up tasks Maintaining and updating the Academic Affairs internal portal and external web pages Assisting in the operations of the Academic and Student Life committee of the Board of Trustees by supporting the preparation of meeting materials, taking minutes, and other matters related to the Board of Trustees as necessary Serving as the academic liaison for commencement and convocation activities, the academic catalogs, academic calendar and Student Handbook Composing general correspondence, memos, credit card statements and reports as requested Partnering with the Office of the President on Board-related topics and other relevant projects Supporting Academic Affairs projects and initiatives related to HL Organizing workflow and developing/overseeing filing systems (paper and digital) Assisting in managing Provost\u2019s budget Providing administrative support for Academic Affairs Director-level searches Facilitating distribution of information for Dean\u2019s List and Delta Sigma Nu honors Maintaining faculty processes by: Administering all full-time faculty contracts including correspondence; ensuring the renewal, recommendation, and annual faculty evaluation processes are completed in compliance with governance Compiling Annual Faculty Evaluation data from deans to report to Human Resources Collecting and maintaining faculty CVs, transcripts and other credentials in alignment with Higher Learning Commission standards Supporting award nomination processes including teaching and Emeriti awards Collaborating with faculty governance committees, including: Promotion and tenure committees for coordination of faculty notifications, dossiers and meetings Student/Faculty Ethics Committee for academic integrity and grade appeal matters General and Cross-Cultural Education Committee for Provost approvals Academic Steering Committee for assistance with documentation and tracking of new program development through Curriculog Faculty Executive Committee for updates to the Faculty Manual and the University Governance and Committees document Reserving meeting spaces for all committees as requested and orders catering as requested for monthly faculty meetings Performs other duties as assigned Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: 5-7 years administrative experience, preferably in higher education, required Technology Skills: Familiarity with common computer software programs, including Microsoft Word, Excel, Teams, and PowerPoint Ability to learn and effectively utilize the computing and workflow processes, scheduling software, etc. Human Relation Skills: Excellent emotional intelligence and interpersonal communication skill Outstanding organizational skills Sound judgment and discretion Ability to maintain confidentiality Excellent problem-solving and analytic abilities Independence, creativity, and a pro-active approach Excellent written and oral communication skills Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission ."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040502848": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Political Science", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Political Science. Job category: Political Science, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Political Science for Spring 2027 Carroll University seeks to hire an Adjunct Instructor of Political Science for Spring 2027. Course: POL274 | International Relations Theory (4 Credits) Section A: Monday, Wednesday and Friday, 12:00 \u2013 1:10 PM This course addresses the core concerns, problems, and issues within International Relations. Most of the course will focus on analyzing and comparing the core theoretical perspectives within International Relations. Additional topics include democratic peace theory, democratization, globalization, understandings of power in the international system, global and regional institutions, and the role of the modern state. Course: POL285 | International Conflict and Security (4 Credits) Section A: Monday, Wednesday and Friday, 10:40 \u2013 11:50 AM The course introduces students to causes and consequences of war and conflict in the international system. The course covers the nature of security and insecurity in the international system and the various ways in which actors (states, lOs, groups and individuals) seek to prevent and mitigate the outbreak of conflict. Among the topics covered include the threat posed by the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction, terrorism, asymmetrical conflict, ethnic conflict, human security, and the challenges these problems present to the international system, states, and individuals. Qualifications: Graduate degree in discipline. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments \u2013 Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. Please indicate the course section letter(s) to which you are applying within your cover letter. Carroll University makes employment opportunities available to qualified individuals whose skills and experience make them suitable candidates for placement. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Carroll University does not discriminate in any manner contrary to law or justice on the basis of race, color, sex, age, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, disability or veteran's status in administration of programs or in the employment of faculty and staff. The employment opportunities list is updated periodically based on openings. Applications will be accepted only for vacancies currently listed. All inquiries will be kept strictly confidential. Graduate Transcript(s) \u2013 Unofficial graduate transcripts will suffice for initial application. However, official graduate transcripts will be required for all final faculty candidates/new hires. To be considered \u201cOfficial,\u201d transcripts must be sent directly from the issuing institution to Carroll University. If you wish to submit official transcripts at time of initial application, please request they be sent electronically to cuaa@carrollu.edu or be sent as a sealed hard copy to the address provided below: Carroll University Attn: Academic Affairs - Voorhees 232 100 N. East Avenue Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040487169": {"title": "Access Services & Circulation Desk Supervisor", "description": "Access Services & Circulation Desk Supervisor. Job category: Library, Staff Employee type: Support Staff. Description At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Access Services Supervisor assists the Library Director in the management, supervision, and administration of the library. This position plays a pivotal role in ensuring access to library resources for students, faculty, and staff. Areas of supervision include library circulation, course reserves, and interlibrary loan. The Access Services Supervisor participates in collection development, acts as a liaison for facilities requests, assists in the creation and implementation of library policy, and assists in the maintenance of the physical collection & space. Responsibilities: \u00b7 \u2022 Supervise the library circulation assistant student workers and provide circulation policies and procedures training and maintenance. \u2022 Troubleshooting: help with resolving technical problems related to access and maintenance of library resources. Work with campus Information Technology Services department to ensure subscribed resources work properly on current hardware and software configurations. \u2022 Maintain configuration in OCLC WorldShare Management System for subscribed resources. \u2022 Integrated Library System: Serve as system administrator for the WMS integrated library system. Provide statistical reporting for usage assessment as needed. \u2022 Manage and update borrowing and lending settings in various ILL software and websites, including OCLC. Create and maintain data, consortia groups and paths, schedules, lending policies, and other information, and access and update as appropriate. \u2022 Train and supervise students to perform bibliographic searching, verify citations, determine holding libraries, and navigate ILL management software and bibliographic tools of moderate complexity. \u2022 Proxy Server and URL Link Resolver: assist with campus EZProxy server and OCLC WorldCat Discovery link resolver. \u2022 Oversee circulation desk operations, including but not limited to answering user questions in person, by phone, or via chat, and enforcing policies and security protocols. \u2022 Plans, organizes, and implements special projects as needed or determined by the Director. \u2022 Perform regular record keeping tasks including tracking patrons and outside organizations with monetary obligations to the library and working with the Business Office and Library Business Manager on collection of library fees. \u2022 Manage staff circulation interface & patron notifications, as well as maintaining the database of current borrowers. \u2022 Coordinate bookstack maintenance and shelving of library materials with library student workers. \u2022 Manage course reserves, including responding to faculty requests, processing materials, and maintaining the online course reserves system. \u2022 Oversee daily interlibrary loan operations, troubleshoot issues, and process materials for lending/borrowing, supervising and training ILL student workers. \u2022 Serve on college committees and participate in professional development opportunities. \u2022 Submit work orders for library projects to the Facilities Department regarding building issues (e.g., repairs, custodial, HVAC, lighting). \u2022 Other duties as assigned Qualifications: Education and/or experience: B.A. or B.S. from accredited institution. M.L.I.S. from accredited institution or 5+ years of experience in a library setting, with knowledge of library functions at an advanced level. Supervisory experience. Experience with integrated library systems. Technology Skills: Proficiency with MS Office Suite, and related computer skills. Familiarity with OCLC\u2019s Worldshare Management Services, including Tipasa, preferred Experience creating & maintaining Springshare LibGuides, preferred Familiarity with Qualtrics XM, preferred Human Relation Skills: Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Ability to multitask, with excellent communication and organizational skills Exceptional customer service skills Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Other Requirements: Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required based on departmental and University needs. Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Avenue, Waukesha, WI 5318"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040484353": {"title": "Assistant Softball Coach", "description": "Assistant Softball Coach. Job category: Athletics Employee type: Part-Time Coach. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Assistant Coach is responsible for assisting the Head Coach in the development and maintenance of an effective and competitive NCAA Division III Softball program at Carroll University. Teaching will include instruction of the skills and strategies necessary for the student-athlete to become competitive and successful in this sport. The recruitment and retention of quality student-athletes in this program will be integral to this position. The Assistant Coach will also be expected to teach and demonstrate the ability to promote the physical, intellectual and social growth of student-athletes. The Assistant Coach is also responsible for assisting the Head Coach in their assigned responsibilities and in the direction of all aspects of their assigned sport program. Responsibilities: Coaching: Understand and comply with all rules and guidelines set forth by the NCAA Division III, Carroll University, CCIW (College Conference of Illinois and Wisconsin), and Carroll University Athletics. Understand and comply with the NCAA and Carroll University philosophy and mission. Act as a professional and positive representative of the Carroll University Educational Experience. Serve as a teacher, counselor, and role model so that student athletes will improve upon their physical, intellectual, and social behaviors. Maximize the physical talent of the team through quality teaching and coaching, and the promotion of teamwork. Assist the Head Coach in planning, organizing and administering all practice sessions. Assist in recruitment of student athletes while working through the Head Coach with the Office of Admission to maximize enrollment. Retain student athletes at Carroll University and within the team. Assist in maintaining, managing and updating equipment inventories. Teach the skills and strategies necessary for the student-athletes\u2019 success in this sport. Assist with the management and maintenance of all game and practice facilities used by the team. Assist in the teaching and coaching of a sub-varsity team Participate in team and departmental camps/clinics, community service projects, fundraising activities, and alumni events whenever possible. Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: High School Diploma/GED required Bachelor\u2019s degree (preferred) Expertise in the sport on intercollegiate level (desired) Previous playing or coaching experience (desired) Teaching, and/or other certifications required for this position, and/or previous teaching experience Human Relations Skills: Ability to function effectively as a team player Excellent oral and written communication skills Sensitivity to the needs of student-athletes and an understanding of their overall educational goals as well as the academic mission of the University Positive attitude towards the University and its intercollegiate programs is essential Ability to conduct self in a productive, professional manner at all times Willingness and ability to support and advance the University Mission Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Special Requirements: Must have valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University\u2019s insurance plan. Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 East Ave., Waukesha, WI 53186. Campus Security Authority Responsibilities This position is designated as a Campus Security Authority in accordance with the Clery Act. This position will be required to report Clery crimes to Public Safety and complete annual training about reporting responsibilities."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040482769": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Business", "description": "Assistant Professor of Business. Job category: Business, Faculty Employee type: Regular Faculty. Full-Time Assistant Professor of Business Full-time Faculty Starting Date: August 23, 2027 Responsibilities: The Assistant Professor of Business will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in general business and management. Additional teaching experience in undergraduate human resources and entrepreneurship is highly desirable. The faculty member will contribute expertise to course and curriculum development efforts within the school and in interdisciplinary endeavors. All Carroll University faculty are expected to participate in professional development activities to enhance teaching effectiveness, to maintain currency in the discipline, and to grow as a leader. In addition, this position will be involved in service to the school and university through representation and meaningful participation on committees, student recruitment and engagement activities, student advising, and assessment programs. Qualifications: Preference will be given to candidates who hold a terminal degree in a business-related discipline and have expertise in introductory business, management, organizational behavior, human resources, and entrepreneurship. Those with a master\u2019s degree and relevant experience will be considered. Teaching experience with online or hybrid modalities preferred. Candidates must have a track record as a successful and effective teacher; prior business or industry experience is highly desired. Proficiency using a wide variety of productivity tools, including Canvas and Microsoft Office, and familiarity with experiential, problem-based, and active learning pedagogical approaches is expected. Rank and Salary: This position is an open-rank, full-time (9-month), Instructional Faculty position. Candidates who have earned advanced rank at another institution will be considered for comparable appointment. The anticipated teaching load is 12-16 credits per semester. Opportunities for summer teaching and project work are available but not guaranteed. Salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications. Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Please submit the following with your application in PDF format: \u00b7 Cover letter and Curriculum Vitae \u00b7 Statement of Teaching Philosophy and Experience which includes the individual\u2019s capacity to teach business management courses. \u00b7 List of three professional references (names and contact information) \u00b7 Teaching evaluations if available Please note: \u00b7 Official transcripts are required for all faculty appointments \u00b7 All positions at Carroll University require a criminal background check The Sullivan School of Business, Engineering and Technology: Established as the Carroll University School of Business in 2017 and renamed The Sullivan School of Business, Engineering and Technology in 2025, the vision of the Sullivan School is to be widely known for excellence in business, engineering and technology education and viewed as a premier education partner by the business community. The Sullivan School attracts and supports faculty who are: \u00b7 Committed to teaching as a primary focus of their academic careers with a dedication to student learning and success. \u00b7 Experienced as teachers, subject matter experts, and professionals within their career fields. \u00b7 Dedicated to grow professionally, advance as leaders, and contribute to both their professions and communities. \u00b7 Credentialed at advanced levels by colleges and universities with exemplary reputations. \u00b7 Engaged in scholarship consistent with the mission of Carroll University and the Sullivan School. \u00b7 Ethical with a strong character serving as the foundation of their professional and personal lives. \u00b7 Appreciative and supportive of workplace diversity represented in thought and in people. \u00b7 Members of the university community that work collaboratively toward a common purpose supportive of individual professional growth. \u00b7 Proud of their affiliation with the university and motivated to advance its mission. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness and includes cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3521 students. The University though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission and ethos. Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040481218": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Honors", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Honors. Job category: Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Honors for Spring 2027 Carroll University seeks to hire an Adjunct Instructor of Honors for Spring 2027. Course: HON252 | Honors Seminar Section A: TBD Qualifications: Graduate degree in discipline. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments \u2013 Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. Please indicate the course section letter(s) to which you are applying within your cover letter. Carroll University makes employment opportunities available to qualified individuals whose skills and experience make them suitable candidates for placement. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Carroll University does not discriminate in any manner contrary to law or justice on the basis of race, color, sex, age, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, disability or veteran's status in administration of programs or in the employment of faculty and staff. The employment opportunities list is updated periodically based on openings. Applications will be accepted only for vacancies currently listed. All inquiries will be kept strictly confidential. Graduate Transcript(s) \u2013 Unofficial graduate transcripts will suffice for initial application. However, official graduate transcripts will be required for all final faculty candidates/new hires. To be considered \u201cOfficial,\u201d transcripts must be sent directly from the issuing institution to Carroll University. If you wish to submit official transcripts at time of initial application, please request they be sent electronically to cuaa@carrollu.edu or be sent as a sealed hard copy to the address provided below: Carroll University Attn: Academic Affairs - Voorhees 232 100 N. East Avenue Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040476356": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Music", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Music. Job category: Music, Adjunct, Music Theory Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Music for Spring 2027 Carroll University seeks to hire an Adjunct Instructor of Music for Spring 2027. Course: MUS112 | Music Theory II (3 Credits) Section A: Monday, Wednesday and Friday, 9:20 \u2013 10:10 AM Study of basic part-writing, harmonic progression, chord identification, figured bass, and Roman numeral analysis of diatonic common-practice music. Qualifications: Graduate degree in discipline. The College: The College of Arts and Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments \u2013 Communication and Sociology; Computational and Physical Sciences; English, Modern Languages and Philosophy; History, Political Sciences and Religious Studies; Life Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3500 students. The University, though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Submission of letters of recommendation, or course evaluations, is encouraged. Please indicate the course section letter(s) to which you are applying within your cover letter. Carroll University makes employment opportunities available to qualified individuals whose skills and experience make them suitable candidates for placement. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Carroll University does not discriminate in any manner contrary to law or justice on the basis of race, color, sex, age, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, disability or veteran's status in administration of programs or in the employment of faculty and staff. The employment opportunities list is updated periodically based on openings. Applications will be accepted only for vacancies currently listed. All inquiries will be kept strictly confidential Graduate Transcript(s) \u2013 Unofficial graduate transcripts will suffice for initial application. However, official graduate transcripts will be required for all final faculty candidates/new hires. To be considered \u201cOfficial,\u201d transcripts must be sent directly from the issuing institution to Carroll University. If you wish to submit official transcripts at time of initial application, please request they be sent electronically to cuaa@carrollu.edu or be sent as a sealed hard copy to the address provided below: Carroll University Attn: Academic Affairs - Voorhees 232 100 N. East Avenue Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040451910": {"title": "Dean - Sullivan School of Business, Engineering & Technology", "description": "Dean - Sullivan School of Business, Engineering & Technology. Job category: School of Business, Staff Employee type: Administrative Staff. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Dean of The Sullivan School of Business, Engineering and Technology (SSBET) serves as the chief academic and administrative officer of the school and provides strategic leadership to advance its mission, enrollment, reputation, and impact. The Dean is responsible for academic quality, faculty leadership, financial stewardship, external partnerships, fundraising, accreditation oversight, and long-range planning. The Dean represents the school to university leadership, alumni, donors, business and industry partners, and the broader community while fostering an innovative, student-centered culture that prepares graduates for successful careers and leadership. Responsibilities: \u2022 Create a supportive teaching and learning environment for students, faculty, and staff. \u2022 Facilitate the annual strategic planning and goal setting process for the SSBET in conjunction with Academic Affairs\u2019 strategic initiatives and Carroll University\u2019s mission, ethos, and strategic plan. \u2022 Engage in successful revenue generation, donor cultivation, and school-wide advancement and marketing in partnership with Institutional Advancement. \u2022 Maintain productive relationships with national and international business communities. \u2022 Lead all external partnerships, including the school\u2019s advisory board. \u2022 Ensure the expansion and development of market-driven global programs across the school. \u2022 Represent the SSBET to the Deans\u2019 Council, Board of Trustees, university community, and external audiences to communicate the school\u2019s capabilities, accomplishments, impact, and aspirations. \u2022 Ensure the school meets or exceeds all accreditation requirements. \u2022 Serve as the SSBET primary representative, spokesperson, and advocate in the school\u2019s student recruitment, retention, and reputation management efforts. \u2022 Recruit, develop, evaluate, and lead the chairs, faculty and staff in the SSBET in a manner that inspires individuals and teams to perform at their best. \u2022 Develop budgets, staffing plans, and collaborative allocation of scarce resources with chairs and departmental faculty. \u2022 Complete annual performance evaluation of chairs, administrators, and staff; collaborating with chairs on performance evaluation of faculty. \u2022 Make recommendations for promotion, tenure and sabbaticals to the relevant faculty governance committees. \u2022 Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications: Education and Experience: \u2022 Doctorate or terminal degree preferred, preferably in a discipline within the school \u2022 20+ years of successful experience in higher education and/or business, with evidence of increased levels of responsibilities of leadership \u2022 An understanding of the business of private higher education \u2022 A strong record of partnerships with external stakeholders \u2022 Experience with change management Human Relations Skills: \u2022 A record of progressive and proven leadership experience \u2022 A strong record of success in attaining external funding through gifts, grants, and partnerships \u2022 Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. \u2022 Evidence of a collaborative leadership and management style that advances goals and programs, evokes trust, and effectively engages the talents, strengths, and experiences of faculty, staff, alumni, and donors \u2022 Outstanding communication skills \u2022 Highly developed relationship-building skills and evidence of strong ties to regional businesses and other stakeholders \u2022 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University Mission Compliance: \u2022 Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Ave, Waukesha, WI 53186 Campus Security Authority Responsibilities: This position is designated as a Campus Security Authority in accordance with the Clery Act. This position will be required to report Clery crimes to Public Safety and complete annual training about reporting responsibilities."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023737812": {"title": "Assistant eSports Coach", "description": "Assistant eSports Coach. Job category: Rec Sports, Staff Employee type: Part-Time Coach. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. This position will assist the head coach in recruiting and coaching duties. The successful coach will be directly involved in recruiting student-players, coaching in-person and online, and building the program identity. This position reports to the Head eSports Coach. The preferred candidate will have the ability to work a flexible schedule, including evenings and weekends. Travel for team tournaments and recruiting may be required. Responsibilities: Assistant the Head Coach to coach and instruct activities during practice and competition Understand and comply with all rules and regulations set forth by Carroll University and eSports competitions Act as a professional and positive representative of the Carroll University Educational Experience. Provide guidance and input on games selected for varsity competition To lead dedicated team practice and games in person. Developing and employing structured practices. Provide care and oversight of equipment and facility Assist with team travel when necessary; including but not limited to driving athletes to competition. Recruit and retain students for the Esports program Support and work camps, clinics and promotional events Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: Associates degree from an accredited college or university preferred. One-year competitive eSports experience involving multiplayer online video games with team-based elements preferred Significant knowledge of one or more of the following games preferred: League of Legends, Overwatch, Valorant, Rocket League, Call of Duty, and/or Smash Bros. Strong relationship-building skills Effective written and oral communication skills, including public speaking Ability to work with a diverse community of students, faculty, administrators and professional colleagues Valid driver\u2019s license and ability to travel required Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Competitive Gaming/Computer Skills: Understanding of computer hardware and software needs related to competitive Esports while collaborating with the Office of Information Technology (OIT). Ability to operate gaming PC and console equipment to meet the needs of an Esports program competing in both formats. Human Relations Skills: \u2022 Excellent oral and written communication skills \u2022 Positive attitude towards the University and its intercollegiate programs is essential \u2022 Ability to conduct self in a productive, professional manner at all times \u2022 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University Mission. Compliance: \u2022 Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Ave, Waukesha, WI 53186 Campus Security Authority Responsibilities: This position is designated as a Campus Security Authority in accordance with the Clery Act. This position will be required to report Clery crimes to Public Safety and complete annual training about reporting responsibilities."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023737811": {"title": "Assistant eSports Coach", "description": "Assistant eSports Coach. Job category: Rec Sports, Staff Employee type: Part-Time Coach. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. This position will assist the head coach in recruiting and coaching duties. The successful coach will be directly involved in recruiting student-players, coaching in-person and online, and building the program identity. This position reports to the Head eSports Coach. The preferred candidate will have the ability to work a flexible schedule, including evenings and weekends. Travel for team tournaments and recruiting may be required. Responsibilities: Assistant the Head Coach to coach and instruct activities during practice and competition Understand and comply with all rules and regulations set forth by Carroll University and eSports competitions Act as a professional and positive representative of the Carroll University Educational Experience. Provide guidance and input on games selected for varsity competition To lead dedicated team practice and games in person. Developing and employing structured practices. Provide care and oversight of equipment and facility Assist with team travel when necessary; including but not limited to driving athletes to competition. Recruit and retain students for the Esports program Support and work camps, clinics and promotional events Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: Associates degree from an accredited college or university preferred. One-year competitive eSports experience involving multiplayer online video games with team-based elements preferred Significant knowledge of one or more of the following games preferred: League of Legends, Overwatch, Valorant, Rocket League, Call of Duty, and/or Smash Bros. Strong relationship-building skills Effective written and oral communication skills, including public speaking Ability to work with a diverse community of students, faculty, administrators and professional colleagues Valid driver\u2019s license and ability to travel required Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Competitive Gaming/Computer Skills: Understanding of computer hardware and software needs related to competitive Esports while collaborating with the Office of Information Technology (OIT). Ability to operate gaming PC and console equipment to meet the needs of an Esports program competing in both formats. Human Relations Skills: \u2022 Excellent oral and written communication skills \u2022 Positive attitude towards the University and its intercollegiate programs is essential \u2022 Ability to conduct self in a productive, professional manner at all times \u2022 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University Mission. Compliance: \u2022 Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Ave, Waukesha, WI 53186 Campus Security Authority Responsibilities: This position is designated as a Campus Security Authority in accordance with the Clery Act. This position will be required to report Clery crimes to Public Safety and complete annual training about reporting responsibilities."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023737810": {"title": "Assistant eSports Coach", "description": "Assistant eSports Coach. Job category: Rec Sports, Staff Employee type: Part-Time Coach. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. This position will assist the head coach in recruiting and coaching duties. The successful coach will be directly involved in recruiting student-players, coaching in-person and online, and building the program identity. This position reports to the Head eSports Coach. The preferred candidate will have the ability to work a flexible schedule, including evenings and weekends. Travel for team tournaments and recruiting may be required. Responsibilities: Assistant the Head Coach to coach and instruct activities during practice and competition Understand and comply with all rules and regulations set forth by Carroll University and eSports competitions Act as a professional and positive representative of the Carroll University Educational Experience. Provide guidance and input on games selected for varsity competition To lead dedicated team practice and games in person. Developing and employing structured practices. Provide care and oversight of equipment and facility Assist with team travel when necessary; including but not limited to driving athletes to competition. Recruit and retain students for the Esports program Support and work camps, clinics and promotional events Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: Associates degree from an accredited college or university preferred. One-year competitive eSports experience involving multiplayer online video games with team-based elements preferred Significant knowledge of one or more of the following games preferred: League of Legends, Overwatch, Valorant, Rocket League, Call of Duty, and/or Smash Bros. Strong relationship-building skills Effective written and oral communication skills, including public speaking Ability to work with a diverse community of students, faculty, administrators and professional colleagues Valid driver\u2019s license and ability to travel required Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Competitive Gaming/Computer Skills: Understanding of computer hardware and software needs related to competitive Esports while collaborating with the Office of Information Technology (OIT). Ability to operate gaming PC and console equipment to meet the needs of an Esports program competing in both formats. Human Relations Skills: \u2022 Excellent oral and written communication skills \u2022 Positive attitude towards the University and its intercollegiate programs is essential \u2022 Ability to conduct self in a productive, professional manner at all times \u2022 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University Mission. Compliance: \u2022 Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Ave, Waukesha, WI 53186 Campus Security Authority Responsibilities: This position is designated as a Campus Security Authority in accordance with the Clery Act. This position will be required to report Clery crimes to Public Safety and complete annual training about reporting responsibilities."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1040449926": {"title": "Building Maintenance Technician II", "description": "Building Maintenance Technician II. Job category: Physical Plant - Maintenance Employee type: Support Staff. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Building Maintenance Technician II provides a skillset within the trades field. The building maintenance technician team provides support for a variety of campus trades needs. Performs a variety of skilled maintenance for campus facilities; ensures that facilities are available for use and consistently maintained in a safe and clean condition. Areas of responsibilities include, but are not limited to: o General Carpentry o Painting o Drywall o Plaster o Cabinetry and Furniture o Window Treatments Repair/Replace o Flooring o Appliance repair o Basic Lock and Door Hardware o Basic Plumbing o Basic Electric o Basic HVAC o General Cleaning Practices \u00b7 Demonstrates strong customer service skills and can establish, maintain, and foster positive and effective working relationships. \u00b7 General practices and operational knowledge of tools used in the maintenance and repair of building facilities including upkeep and maintenance of tools. \u00b7 Occupational hazards and standard safety practices. \u00b7 Principles and practices of recordkeeping and facilities maintenance software. \u00b7 Identifies and reports life safety and maintenance needs to the supervisor. \u00b7 Comply with University facility standards of conduct. \u00b7 Accomplishing work orders through our facility maintenance software on a daily basis. \u00b7 Maintain and manage records on fixed asset expenditures \u00b7 Assist with project work scopes for building maintenance projects. Remove snow and ice from stairs, walks and ramps including shoveling, salting and plowing. \u00b7 Perform other duties as assigned by your supervisor. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience \u00b7 High School Diploma/GED required \u00b7 Trade school, apprenticeship, or other formal training preferred \u00b7 3 - 5 years of experience in a specific trade preferred or general maintenance, preference of locksmith experience or the ability to complete required locksmith training within 1 year of hire \u00b7 Working familiarity with standard building codes Human Relations Skills \u00b7 Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with diverse constituencies (internal and external) \u00b7 Strong written and verbal communication skills \u00b7 Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Technology Skills \u00b7 PC proficient; experience with Office 365 preferred \u00b7 Understanding of Word, Excel, and Power Point \u00b7 Working knowledge and proficiency with maintenance management system preferred Physical Demands \u00b7 Ability to stoop, kneel, reach, and inspect work sites. \u00b7 Sufficient strength and dexterity to perform duties and responsibilities of the position \u00b7 Job requires the ability to lift, carry, push, and pull materials and objects weighing up to 100 pounds or heavier weights with assistance and/or the use of proper equipment. Environmental Elements \u00b7 Work in both indoor and outdoor environments with moderate to loud noise levels. \u00b7 May work in controlled temperature conditions, cold and hot temperatures, or inclement weather conditions. \u00b7 Exposure to chemicals, mechanical and electrical hazards, and hazardous physical substances and fumes. Other Requirements \u00b7 Must have valid driver\u2019s license and be insurable under the university\u2019s insurance plan. \u00b7 Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs \u00b7 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University Ethos Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 East Ave., Waukesha, WI 53186."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023725893": {"title": "Director of Business Technology Solutions", "description": "Director of Business Technology Solutions. Job category: Information Technology, Staff Employee type: Administrative Staff. At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Director of Business Technology Solutions provides strategic leadership for aligning technology solutions, business processes, and data with Carroll University\u2019s institutional priorities. This role leads Business Analysis, Application Development, Project Delivery, Enterprise Applications, and Data & Analytics, partnering across the University to improve processes, deliver effective solutions, and advance strategic initiatives. Responsibilities: Provide strategic leadership for Business Technology Solutions, ensuring technology initiatives, business processes, data, and solutions align with University priorities and goals. Lead and develop teams responsible for Business Analysis, Application Development, Project Delivery, Enterprise Applications, and Data & Analytics, fostering collaboration, accountability, and continuous improvement. Partner with University leaders and campus stakeholders to understand business needs, identify opportunities, and translate strategic and operational priorities into effective technology solutions. Lead business process analysis and improvement efforts, evaluating people, process, data, and technology to improve efficiency, effectiveness, and the campus experience. Provide thought leadership on technology, AI, emerging technologies, and innovative solutions that support institutional transformation and strategic objectives. Oversee the evaluation, selection, implementation, integration, and optimization of enterprise applications and technology solutions. Establish and promote standards, governance, and best practices for solution delivery, application development, business analysis, data, and project execution. Oversee the effectiveness and continued evolution of enterprise applications, integrations, and technology solutions, identifying opportunities to improve, modernize, consolidate, or replace existing capabilities. Partner with technical and functional teams, vendors, and other stakeholders to deliver secure, scalable, and sustainable solutions. Provide leadership for data and analytics capabilities that strengthen data accessibility, governance, reporting, and data-informed decision-making across the University. Oversee the prioritization and delivery of technology initiatives, balancing institutional priorities, business value, resource capacity, risk, and dependencies. Build strong relationships across the University and serve as a trusted technology partner and advisor to campus leadership and stakeholders. Collaborate with OIT Leadership team to review and maintain strategic roadmaps for business technology capabilities, enterprise systems, and data. Perform additional duties as required. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: Bachelor\u2019s degree in information technology, Information Systems, Computer Science, Business, or a related field required; equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience may be considered. Minimum of ten years of progressively responsible experience in information technology, business technology, enterprise applications, solution delivery, or a related field. Minimum of five years of leadership experience managing and developing technology professionals and/or multidisciplinary technology teams. Demonstrated experience leading complex technology initiatives and delivering enterprise solutions that support organizational strategy and business needs. Experience partnering with business and organizational leaders to evaluate processes, identify opportunities, define requirements, and translate business needs into effective technology solutions. Strong understanding of enterprise applications, application development and integration, data and analytics, project delivery methodologies, and modern technology architectures. Experience evaluating, selecting, implementing, integrating, and optimizing enterprise technology solutions and vendor-provided platforms. Experience developing strong partnerships with technical teams, business stakeholders, vendors, and organizational leadership. Knowledge of emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence, automation, cloud technologies, and evolving approaches to enterprise technology and solution delivery. Higher education experience preferred but not required. Technical Skills: Strong understanding of enterprise technology ecosystems, including ERP and other enterprise applications, application development, systems integration, data and analytics, and cloud-based technologies. Conceptual knowledge of modern application and data architecture, including APIs and integrations, relational databases, data management, reporting and analytics technologies, data governance, and identity and access management. Knowledge in software development lifecycle (SDLC), application lifecycle management, and solution delivery methodologies. (Agile, Waterfall, Hybrid) Understanding artificial intelligence, automation, and emerging technologies, with the ability to evaluate their potential business value, risks, and appropriate application. Experience using project management software. Human Relations Skills: Exceptional communication, analytical, interpersonal, and relationship-building skills, with the ability to effectively engage technical and non-technical audiences at all levels of the University. Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background or job type. Demonstrated leadership experience building, motivating, developing, and empowering high-performing teams while fostering accountability, collaboration, and professional growth. Ability to build trusted partnerships, influence decision-making, navigate differing perspectives, and develop consensus across diverse stakeholder groups. Demonstrated ability to lead teams and stakeholders through organizational, process, and technology change while encouraging innovation, adaptability, and continuous improvement. Strong leadership experience in building, motivating, developing and managing employees. Ability to develop, nurture and encourage staff to embrace change and move the institution forward. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Ave, Waukesha, WI 53186"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023725892": {"title": "Sr. Data Analyst", "description": "Sr. Data Analyst. Job category: Information Technology Services, Staff Employee type: Administrative Staff. At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Senior Data Analyst is a strategic and technical role within Information Technology responsible for advancing the institution\u2019s data analytics, reporting, and decision-support capabilities. This position partners closely with academic and administrative departments to analyze data, develop actionable insights, improve data quality, and support data-informed decision-making across the institution. The Senior Data Analyst designs and develops enterprise reporting solutions, dashboards, data models, and analytics frameworks while helping establish data governance standards and best practices. This role serves as a bridge between business stakeholders and technical teams to ensure institutional data is accurate, accessible, secure, and aligned with strategic goals. The position requires strong analytical, communication, and problem-solving skills, along with experience working with ERP systems, data visualization tools, SQL, and modern analytics platforms in a collaborative higher education environment. Responsibilities : Design, develop, and maintain enterprise dashboards, reports, and data visualizations for academic and administrative departments. Translate complex business requirements into meaningful analytics and reporting solutions. Support institutional data governance initiatives, including data quality, integrity, stewardship, and standardization efforts. Partner with stakeholders to establish consistent reporting definitions, business rules, and data standards. Assist in maintaining enterprise data models, data dictionaries, and metadata documentation. Promote responsible and secure use of institutional data in alignment with compliance and privacy requirements. Assist departments seeking to improve operational effectiveness through data insights. Assist with prioritizing analytics initiatives. Work with enterprise systems including ERP/SIS, CRM, HRIS, LMS, and other institutional applications to support data integration and reporting. Develop and optimize SQL queries, data pipelines, and automated reporting processes. Support analytics and business intelligence platforms such as Power BI, or similar tools. Assist in evaluating emerging analytics, AI, and data technologies that improve institutional effectiveness. Identify opportunities to improve reporting efficiency, automation, and data accessibility. Participate in cross-functional IT and institutional projects related to data, reporting, and process improvement. Complete other duties as assigned. Qualifications : Education and/or Experience: \u00b7 Bachelor\u2019s degree in Information Systems, Computer Science, Data Analytics, Business, Mathematics, Statistics, or related field. \u00b7 5+ years of experience in data analytics, business intelligence, institutional research, or related analytics roles. \u00b7 Experience working in higher education environments, desired. \u00b7 Strong experience with SQL and relational databases. \u00b7 Strong analytical, problem-solving, and communication skills. \u00b7 Ability to explain technical concepts and analytics insights to non-technical audiences. \u00b7 Experience supporting institutional effectiveness, enrollment analytics, student success, or operational reporting initiatives. Technical Skills: \u00b7 Experience developing dashboards and reports using Power BI, Tableau, or similar BI tools. \u00b7 Experience working with enterprise applications such as ERP, SIS, CRM, or HRIS systems. \u00b7 Familiarity with Ellucian, Anthology, Workday, Salesforce, or similar higher education platforms. \u00b7 Experience with data warehousing, ETL/integration processes, or cloud analytics platforms. \u00b7 Knowledge of data governance, compliance, and privacy practices. \u00b7 Exposure to predictive analytics, AI, or machine learning concepts. Human Relations Skills: \u00b7 Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. \u00b7 Effective interpersonal and communication skills with the ability to collaborate across teams and contribute to problem-solving efforts. \u00b7 Clear and professional written and verbal communication skills suited for both technical and non-technical audiences. \u00b7 Strong commitment to delivering responsive and respectful customer service. \u00b7 Ability to stay focused, organized, and productive when working under deadlines or during periods of high activity. \u00b7 Adaptable and able to manage shifting priorities in a dynamic work environment. \u00b7 Skilled in managing multiple assignments and projects simultaneously with minimal oversight. \u00b7 Equally comfortable working independently or as part of a collaborative team. \u00b7 Demonstrated ability to build and maintain positive working relationships with a wide range of colleagues and stakeholders. \u00b7 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Compliance: \u00b7 Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Ave., Waukesha, WI 5318 This position has a hybrid work arrangement - 3 days in office, 2 days remote."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023724992": {"title": "Assistant Men's Lacrosse Coach", "description": "Assistant Men's Lacrosse Coach. Job category: Athletics Employee type: Part-Time Coach. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population . The Assistant Coach is responsible for assisting the Head Coach in the development and maintenance of an effective and competitive NCAA Division III Men\u2019s Lacrosse program at Carroll University. Teaching will include instruction of the skills and strategies necessary for the student-athlete to become competitive and successful in this sport. The recruitment and retention of quality student-athletes in this program will be integral to this position. The Assistant Coach will also be expected to teach and demonstrate the ability to promote the physical, intellectual and social growth of student-athletes. The Assistant Coach is also responsible for assisting the Head Coach in their assigned responsibilities and in the direction of all aspects of their assigned sport program Responsibilities: Coaching: Understand and comply with all rules and guidelines set forth b y the NCAA Division III , Carroll University, CCIW (College Conference of Illinois and Wisconsin), and Carroll University Athletics. Understand and compl y with the NCAA and Carroll University philosophy and mission. Act as a professional and positive representative of the Carroll University Educational Experience. Serve as a teacher, counselor, and role model so that student athletes will improve upon their physical, intellectual, and social behaviors. Maximize the physical talent of the team through quality teaching and coaching, and the promotion of teamwork. Assist the Head Coach in planning, organizing and administering all practice sessions. Assist in recruitment of student athletes while work ing through the Head Coach with the Office of Admission to maximize enrollment. Retain student athletes at Carroll University and within the team. Assist in m aintain ing , manag ing and updat ing equipment inventories. Teach the skills and strategies necessary for the student-athletes\u2019 success in this sport. Assist with the management and maintenance of all game and practice facilities used by the team. Assist in the teaching and coaching of a sub-varsity team. Participate in team and departmental camps/clinics, community service projects, fundraising activities, and alumni events whenever possible. Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: Bachelor\u2019s degree required Expertise in the sport on intercollegiate level (desired) Previous playing or coaching experience (desired) Teaching, and/or other certifications required for this position, and/or previous teaching experience Human Relations Skills: Ability to function effectively as a team player Excellent oral and written communication skills Sensitivity to the needs of student -athlete s and an understanding of their overall educational goals as well as the academic mission of the University Positive attitude towards the University and its intercollegiate programs is essential Ability to conduct self in a productive, professional manner at all times Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Special Requirements: Must have valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University\u2019s insurance plan. Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 208 Wright St., Waukesha, WI 53186. Campus Security Authority Responsibilities This position is designated as a Campus Security Authority in accordance with the Clery Act. This position will be required to report Clery crimes to Public Safety and complete annual training about reporting responsibilities."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023721921": {"title": "Assistant Baseball Coach", "description": "Assistant Baseball Coach. Job category: Athletics Employee type: Part-Time Coach. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Assistant Coach is responsible for assisting the Head Coach in the development and maintenance of an effective and competitive NCAA Division III Baseball program at Carroll University. Teaching will include instruction of the skills and strategies necessary for the student-athlete to become competitive and successful in this sport. The recruitment and retention of quality student-athletes in this program will be integral to this position. The Assistant Coach will also be expected to teach and demonstrate the ability to promote the physical, intellectual and social growth of student-athletes. The Assistant Coach is also responsible for assisting the Head Coach in their assigned responsibilities and in the direction of all aspects of their assigned sport program. Responsibilities: Coaching: Understand and comply with all rules and guidelines set forth by the NCAA Division III, Carroll University, CCIW (College Conference of Illinois and Wisconsin), and Carroll University Athletics. Understand and comply with the NCAA and Carroll University philosophy and mission. Act as a professional and positive representative of the Carroll University Educational Experience. Serve as a teacher, counselor, and role model so that student athletes will improve upon their physical, intellectual, and social behaviors. Maximize the physical talent of the team through quality teaching and coaching, and the promotion of teamwork. Assist the Head Coach in planning, organizing and administering all practice sessions. Assist in recruitment of student athletes while working through the Head Coach with the Office of Admission to maximize enrollment. Retain student athletes at Carroll University and within the team. Assist in maintaining, managing and updating equipment inventories. Teach the skills and strategies necessary for the student-athletes\u2019 success in this sport. Assist with the management and maintenance of all game and practice facilities used by the team. Assist in the teaching and coaching of a sub-varsity team. Participate in team and departmental camps/clinics, community service projects, fundraising activities, and alumni events whenever possible. Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: High School Diploma/GED required Bachelor\u2019s degree (preferred) Expertise in the sport on intercollegiate level (desired) Previous playing or coaching experience (desired) Human Relations Skills: Ability to function effectively as a team player Excellent oral and written communication skills Sensitivity to the needs of student-athletes and an understanding of their overall educational goals as well as the academic mission of the University Positive attitude towards the University and its intercollegiate programs is essential Ability to conduct self in a productive, professional manner at all times Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Special Requirements: Must have valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University\u2019s insurance plan. Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 East Ave., Waukesha, WI 53186. Campus Security Authority Responsibilities This position is designated as a Campus Security Authority in accordance with the Clery Act. This position will be required to report Clery crimes to Public Safety and complete annual training about reporting responsibilities"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023700161": {"title": "Pioneer Shop Sales Associate", "description": "Pioneer Shop Sales Associate. Job category: Auxiliary Services, Staff Employee type: Support Staff. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. This position is responsible for providing excellent customer service to the Pioneer Shop and supporting store operations in the manager\u2019s absence. Responsibilities will include merchandising, operating the cash register, shelving and restocking, inventory management, etc. Responsibilities: \u2022Open and close the store independently \u2022Greet customers and assist with questions and merchandise purchases \u2022Accurately perform cashier duties including handling cash, checks and credit card transactions and internal department purchases \u2022Shelve, arrange, clean, and organize merchandise to maintain an appealing sales floor \u2022Assist with merchandise inventory and product processing \u2022Support textbook and course material rental process; assist with questions and course material delivery to students as needed \u2022Support Manager with financial and inventory reporting, as requested \u2022Perform other duties as assigned in support of department and/or division needs and goals. \u2022Serve as a backup to other staff members in their absence \u2022Provide supervisory support to student employees in absence of Manager Qualifications: Education and /or Experience: \u2022High School degree or GED \u20221+ year experience in retail preferred \u2022Strong organization skills and attention to detail required Technology Skills: \u2022Thorough knowledge and use of MS Office applications required \u2022Ability to use and maintain voice mail and e-mail required Human Relations Skills: \u2022Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. \u2022Excellent customer service and communication skills both in writing and in person \u2022Ability to work independently and with a team \u2022Ability to work with diverse constituencies \u2022Excellent interpersonal skills and ability to build relationships with other University departments and constituents \u2022Ability to maintain confidentiality Physical Demands: \u2022Must be able to lift up to 50 lbs. \u2022Must be able to sit/stand for extended periods of time \u2022Sufficient strength, physical dexterity, and ability to perform duties and responsibilities of position Other Requirements \u2022Valid Wisconsin driver\u2019s license and insurable under the University\u2019s insurance plan \u2022Ability to work evenings, weekends and holidays as required \u2022Must be flexible in schedule and work well under pressure \u2022Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 East Ave., Waukesha, WI 53186."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023692166": {"title": "Director of Reservations and Event Services", "description": "Director of Reservations and Event Services. Job category: Reservations and Event Services, Staff Employee type: Administrative Staff. Description: At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. The Director of Reservations and Event Services provides the leadership, vision and oversight necessary to ensure the successful management, marketing, budgeting, and coordination of events required to deliver outstanding service, achieve budgetary goals and sustain a positive work environment for all employees. Director of Reservations and Event Services Responsibilities: Database management \u2022 Develop, manage and maintain the university database for all events with scheduling software; maintain client database, events calendar, and create appropriate reports \u2022 Manage and assign user access and ensure data security and integrity \u2022 Coordinate and schedule requests to use university facilities in accordance with established institutional policies and procedures \u2022 Serve as the primary event scheduler, including oversight of data standards and account maintenance \u2022 Provide expert training to faculty, staff, student organization officers, and the general student body \u2022 Create and analyze reports as needed for benchmarking, establish metrics and make recommendations regarding institutional policies and procedures Supervision \u2022 Directly supervise and evaluate all professional staff, student office staff, and other staff as required \u2022 Assist with recruitment and selection of professional and student staff \u2022 Provide instruction and training for professional and student staff as required Administrative \u2022 Serve as the primary contact for non-university client requests to use university facilities; negotiate and execute appropriate facilities agreements and ensure compliance with accounting and risk management requirements, as well as local, state and federal regulations. \u2022 Utilizing the university's event scheduling software, coordinate reservations and services for all scheduled events; provide oversight for the scheduling and use of all university facilities. \u2022 Collaborate with the Senior Director to plan and execute appropriate marketing strategies, including market research, communication, sales development, and special programs, required to achieve university and budgetary goals; develop and produce appropriate publications, website content and promotional materials; identify opportunities and develop materials for inclusion in appropriate newsletters, brochures, and other publications. \u2022 Oversee summer camp and conference operations and provide support for major campus events, including orientation, move-in, homecoming, holiday events and commencement. \u2022 Develop and maintain the university's facilities use policies, procedures, and guidelines \u2022 Make policy recommendations regarding new and innovative programs to both enhance financial performance and enhance/expand services to university and non-university clients. \u2022 Conduct post-event research to monitor client satisfaction and identify areas for service enhancement. \u2022 Develop and manage annual operating budget; propose annual fee schedules for facility use and services to ensure budget projections are met. \u2022 Ensure timely submission of invoices to the university business office. Produce monthly budget reports and distribute internal invoices to non-university clients. \u2022 Identify and participate in ongoing professional development, continuing education and training as required; play an active role in industry associations such as ACCED-I, NACAS, ACUI and/or other appropriate groups. \u2022 Provide evening and weekend coverage for scheduled events as required \u2022 Serve as a back-up to other staff members in their absence \u2022 Perform other duties as assigned in support of department and/or division needs and goals. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience: \u2022 Bachelor's degree in business, marketing, hospitality management or related field is required; master's degree is preferred. \u2022 Minimum of 5-10 years of experience in event planning, conference services, marketing or related field is required; experience in higher education is preferred. \u2022 Earned or anticipated CCEP, CASP and/or CMP certification is preferred. \u2022 Higher education work experience with knowledge of best practices is preferred \u2022 Experience with event scheduling software and survey tools such as Qualtrics is preferred \u2022 Experience in budget management and familiarity with generally accepted accounting principles is required; operations and contract administration experience is required. Technology Skills: \u2022 Experience with enterprise and database management software required. \u2022 Thorough knowledge of MS Office applications required. \u2022 Use of voice mail and e-mail required. \u2022 Experience with appropriate software applications, including reservations systems required. \u2022 General understanding of client-server computing and networking preferred. \u2022 Familiarity with basic graphic and web design and editing tools preferred. Human Relations Skills: \u2022 Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. \u2022 Attention to detail. \u2022 Strong communication, management and administrative skills. \u2022 Ability to maintain confidentiality. \u2022 High customer service orientation. \u2022 Ability to work as a team member and to work with a diverse group of constituents Physical Demands: \u2022 Ability to stand, sit or walk for extended periods of time. \u2022 Ability to lift and carry 50 pounds. Other: \u2022 Ability to work evenings, weekends and holidays as needed \u2022 Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Compliance: o Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 208 Wright St., Waukesha, WI 53186."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023692101": {"title": "Custodian", "description": "Custodian. Job category: Physical Plant Employee type: Support Staff. Responsibilities: Demonstrate proper and effective cleaning methods in accordance with the quality control checklist Clean, sweep, vacuum, mop, scrub and wax floors, stairs and other surfaces Clean and service lavatories and shower room facilities Use light and heavy duty cleaning equipment and attachments Report items and equipment that needs repair (doors, furniture, lights, and faucets, etc.) Follow and comply with all safety and work rules and regulations, opens and closes, locks and unlocks facilities as needed Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Educational and/or Experience High school graduate (diploma/GED) preferred Knowledge of proper use and disposal of hazardous chemicals and products Knowledge of bodily fluid cleanup and proper protection Knowledge of all floor types, such as wood, vinyl and ceramic tile as well as carpet fibers, weaves and backings Three years of experience in a related field preferred Human Relations Skills Ability to be flexible and prioritize work in a consistently changing environment. Establish and maintain working relationships with employees, staff, and faculty and campus visitors. Follow oral and/or written instructions. Communicate effectively in both oral and written form. Ability to work as part of a team and independently as required. Physical Demands Considerable physical activity is required. Requires heavy physical work; heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling required of objects up to 50 pounds. Physical work is primary part (more than 70%) of job. Work environment involves some exposure to hazards or physical risks, which require following basic safety precautions. Work may involve moderate exposure to unusual elements, such as extreme temperatures, dirt, dust, fumes, smoke, unpleasant odors, and/or loud noises. Work in all types of weather and temperatures both indoors (temperature variation in campus buildings) and outdoors (precipitation and various temperatures). Frequent and repetitive bending, twisting, reaching, pushing, and pulling are required. Use proper lifting (up to 15 pounds) and safety techniques while climbing ladders. Other Requirements Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs Valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University's insurance plan preferred Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission ."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023692100": {"title": "Custodian", "description": "Custodian. Job category: Physical Plant, Staff Employee type: Support Staff. Responsibilities: Demonstrate proper and effective cleaning methods in accordance with the quality control checklist Clean, sweep, vacuum, mop, scrub and wax floors, stairs and other surfaces Clean and service lavatories and shower room facilities Use light and heavy duty cleaning equipment and attachments Report items and equipment that needs repair (doors, furniture, lights, and faucets, etc.) Follow and comply with all safety and work rules and regulations, opens and closes, locks and unlocks facilities as needed Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Educational and/or Experience High school graduate (diploma/GED) preferred Knowledge of proper use and disposal of hazardous chemicals and products Knowledge of bodily fluid cleanup and proper protection Knowledge of all floor types, such as wood, vinyl and ceramic tile as well as carpet fibers, weaves and backings Three years of experience in a related field preferred Human Relations Skills Ability to be flexible and prioritize work in a consistently changing environment. Establish and maintain working relationships with employees, staff, and faculty and campus visitors. Follow oral and/or written instructions. Communicate effectively in both oral and written form. Ability to work as part of a team and independently as required. Physical Demands Considerable physical activity is required. Requires heavy physical work; heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling required of objects up to 50 pounds. Physical work is primary part (more than 70%) of job. Work environment involves some exposure to hazards or physical risks, which require following basic safety precautions. Work may involve moderate exposure to unusual elements, such as extreme temperatures, dirt, dust, fumes, smoke, unpleasant odors, and/or loud noises. Work in all types of weather and temperatures both indoors (temperature variation in campus buildings) and outdoors (precipitation and various temperatures). Frequent and repetitive bending, twisting, reaching, pushing, and pulling are required. Use proper lifting (up to 15 pounds) and safety techniques while climbing ladders. Other Requirements Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs Valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University's insurance plan preferred Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission ."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1023693186": {"title": "Custodian", "description": "Custodian. Job category: Physical Plant, Staff Employee type: Support Staff. Responsibilities: Demonstrate proper and effective cleaning methods in accordance with the quality control checklist Clean, sweep, vacuum, mop, scrub and wax floors, stairs and other surfaces Clean and service lavatories and shower room facilities Use light and heavy duty cleaning equipment and attachments Report items and equipment that needs repair (doors, furniture, lights, and faucets, etc.) Follow and comply with all safety and work rules and regulations, opens and closes, locks and unlocks facilities as needed Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Educational and/or Experience High school graduate (diploma/GED) preferred Knowledge of proper use and disposal of hazardous chemicals and products Knowledge of bodily fluid cleanup and proper protection Knowledge of all floor types, such as wood, vinyl and ceramic tile as well as carpet fibers, weaves and backings Three years of experience in a related field preferred Human Relations Skills Ability to be flexible and prioritize work in a consistently changing environment. Establish and maintain working relationships with employees, staff, and faculty and campus visitors. Follow oral and/or written instructions. Communicate effectively in both oral and written form. Ability to work as part of a team and independently as required. Physical Demands Considerable physical activity is required. Requires heavy physical work; heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling required of objects up to 50 pounds. Physical work is primary part (more than 70%) of job. Work environment involves some exposure to hazards or physical risks, which require following basic safety precautions. Work may involve moderate exposure to unusual elements, such as extreme temperatures, dirt, dust, fumes, smoke, unpleasant odors, and/or loud noises. Work in all types of weather and temperatures both indoors (temperature variation in campus buildings) and outdoors (precipitation and various temperatures). Frequent and repetitive bending, twisting, reaching, pushing, and pulling are required. Use proper lifting (up to 15 pounds) and safety techniques while climbing ladders. Other Requirements Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs Valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University's insurance plan preferred Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission ."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1006957194": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Graduate Education", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Graduate Education. Job category: Graduate Education, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor for EDU672 and EDU673"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1006921804": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy. Job category: Physical Therapy, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy Starting date: Fall 2026 Carroll University seeks an Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy for the Fall 2026 semester. The Department of Physical Therapy in the College of Health Sciences offers a Doctor of Physical Therapy. Course: PTH5405 Introduction to Patient Management Lab Time/Days: Wednesday, 8:00AM-11:20AM & 11:30AM-2:50PM Brief Course Description : This course introduces the nervous system and how behavior is produced from cellular changes in the brain. The class will focus on understanding the fundamental anatomy and physiology involved in the pathways that connect neural activity with coordinated muscle movement. We will also cover relevant embryology, higher cognitive functions, and disease states. Course: PTH5422: Clinical Anatomy Palpation Lab Time/Days: Wednesday, 9:00AM-10:50AM & 12:00PM-1:50PM Brief Course Description : This course presents a comprehensive consideration of human anatomy including both neuromusculoskeletal components and internal organ systems. Systems include musculoskeletal, neurological, genitourinary, gastrointestinal, skeletal, and cardiopulmonary. Biomechanical function, topographic and radiographic correlations, and clinical applications are emphasized. In-depth understanding of the gross anatomy of the human body is obtained through lecture, audiovisual, computer, and gross cadaver prosecution. Introductory mapping and palpation exercises will also be integrated into this course. Qualifications: Doctorate or Master of Physical Therapy. Official transcripts due upon hire. The Program: The Doctor of Physical Therapy Program at Carroll University is accredited by the Commission on Accreditation in Physical Therapy Education (CAPTE), 1111 North Fairfax Street, Alexandria, Virginia 22314; telephone: 703-706-3245; email: accreditation@apta.org; website: capteonline.org. The College: The College of Health Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments; Public Health, Athletic Training, Physician Assistant Studies, Occupational Therapy, Physical Therapy, Nursing, and Department of Movement Sciences. The mission of the College of Health Science is to educate students to be innovative professionals who collaborate to serve the health needs of their communities. The College of Health Sciences currently accounts for ~60% of university enrollment. A dynamic and growing entity, College-wide foci include interprofessional education and practice, the integrated approach to health, innovative learning and healthcare technologies, community partnerships and service to medically underserved populations. To support these initiatives the University has renovated a medical arts building to house and support its service learning and Community Health Service programs and partnered with the Waukesha Free Clinic at Carroll University. The College supports the interdisciplinary nature of health science education and has received federal and private foundation grant awards to support these, as well as program-specific initiatives. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3521 students. The University though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV and examples of teaching effectiveness. If you have past course evaluations, please submit several that demonstrate your teaching effectiveness. In lieu of course evaluations, a letter from your current supervisor attesting to your skills in the classroom environment. Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=990181252": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant", "description": "Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant. Job category: Physician Assistant, Faculty Employee type: Regular Faculty. Description : Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant Studies Starting Date : August 24, 2026 Carroll University seeks a Clinical Assistant Professor of Physician Assistant Studies for its innovative Master of Science in Physician Assistant Studies (MSPAS) program. In addition to traditional Physician Assistant studies, the program is dedicated to developing students' competency to serve medically underserved populations and facilitate interprofessional healthcare practice. Candidates who are innovative and value a dynamic and comprehensive approach to curriculum, instruction, and physician assistant practice are encouraged to apply. This is a 12-month, full time position. Qualifications: Carroll University (www.carrollu.edu) invites applications for the position of Clinical Assistant Professor of the Master of Physician Assistant Studies Program. Candidates will: \u00b7 Be an NCCPA board certified physician assistant \u00b7 Hold a terminal degree in physician assistant studies (MSPAS or MPAS) \u00b7 Hold current physician assistant licensure or be eligible for licensure \u00b7 Possess a minimum of 5 years of clinical experience\u2014primary care experience preferred \u00b7 Possess excellent interpersonal and organizational skills \u00b7 Proficiency with on-line student tracking systems and test question data banks is desired Responsibilities : The clinical faculty line support teaching, scholarship and professional activity, and service. This position is focused on supporting the clinical education component of the program, the candidate may be asked to participate in didactic teaching dependent on program needs. The position reports to the Program Director and works under the direction of the Director of Clinical Education with the development, implementation, and evaluation of the Program\u2019s clinical curriculum and supervised clinical practice experiences (SCPEs). Duties include but not limited to: \u00b7 Recruit, develop, and maintain clinical year rotation sites. \u00b7 Coordinate student placements. \u00b7 Perform site visits to evaluate as part of their ongoing assessment. \u00b7 Evaluate and track student performance, progression, and provide remedial instruction when necessary. \u00b7 Assist with activities and assessments such as End of Rotation Exams, OSCEs, workshops, and simulations. \u00b7 Didactic teaching dependent on program needs and candidate expertise/interest \u00b7 Attend faculty program and department committee meetings, participate in service, scholarship, and professional development. \u00b7 Perform other duties as assigned. Professional activity and service may include, but is not limited to, university committees, programmatic projects, academic advising, admissions support, clinical practice within our program at the Waukesha Free Clinic at Carroll University, serving as a preceptor to our students. Candidates who are innovative and value a dynamic and comprehensive approach to curriculum, instruction, and physician assistant practice in an interdisciplinary environment are encouraged to apply. Rank and Salary: This is a clinical faculty track position at the rank of clinical assistant professor. Salary is commensurate with experience. The Program: The Physician Assistant Studies Program educates physician assistants to provide comprehensive quality health care to all, to be respectful of patient values, to be committed to ethical principles, and to be grounded in evidence-based practice and clinical reasoning. Graduates contribute to both their profession and their communities and are prepared to practice medicine in a variety of primary care settings. Graduates are prepared to provide service to medically underserved communities and diverse patient populations. Carroll University has developed a partnership with the Waukesha Free Clinic located at the Carroll University Community Health Services building where our PA students have the exclusive privileges. The MSPAS program is accredited by the Accreditation Review Commission on Education for the Physician Assistant (ARC-PA) providing education to 32 students/year. The program has a strong commitment to inter-disciplinary team building and faculty include PA-Cs, PhDs, and MDs. The program is moving to the new 61,000 sq. ft. interdisciplinary College of Health Science building on Carroll\u2019s main campus in the fall of 2026. The facility includes a dedicated Physician Assistant teaching wing which includes didactic teaching space, 10 private simulation exam rooms, 2 hospital simulation rooms, faculty offices, and student and faculty lounge spaces. In addition, the program utilizes the Michael and Mary Jaharis Laboratories for its cadaver lab and Anatomage Dissection table. The College : The College of Health Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments\u2014 Athletic Training, Human Movement Sciences, Interprofessional Studies, Nursing, Occupational Therapy, Physical Therapy, and Physician Assistant Studies. Health sciences account for ~60% of the University enrollment. The mission of the College of Health Science is to educate students to be innovative professionals who collaborate to serve the health needs of their communities. A dynamic and growing entity, College wide foci include interprofessional education and practice, the integrated approach to health, innovative learning and healthcare technologies, community partnerships and service to medically underserved populations. The new interdisciplinary, 61,000 sq. ft., College of Health Science building, opening in the fall of 2026, provides state of the art teaching, simulation, and patient treatment spaces. This building houses our graduate Exercise Physiology, Occupational Therapy, Pathologist Assistant, and Physician Assistant programs. While the 55,000 sq. ft. Center for Graduate Studies houses our graduate Athletic Training and Doctor of Physical Therapy Programs, and the Hastad and Henke facilities support Nursing. The Carroll University Community Health Services building expands education opportunities, while providing valuable pro bono services for the local community. The College supports the interdisciplinary nature of health science education and has received federal and private foundation grant awards to support these as well as program specific initiatives. The University : Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive University. The Carroll mission is to \u201cprovide a superior education, rooted in its Presbyterian and liberal arts heritage, and draws upon the Christian tradition to prepare all students for vocational success, lifelong learning and service in a diverse and global society.\u201d Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves 3200 undergraduate and graduate students. The University though intentionally ecumenical and non-sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=990162176": {"title": "Custodian", "description": "Custodian. Job category: Physical Plant Employee type: Support Staff. Responsibilities: Demonstrate proper and effective cleaning methods in accordance with the quality control checklist Clean, sweep, vacuum, mop, scrub and wax floors, stairs and other surfaces Clean and service lavatories and shower room facilities Use light and heavy duty cleaning equipment and attachments Report items and equipment that needs repair (doors, furniture, lights, and faucets, etc.) Follow and comply with all safety and work rules and regulations, opens and closes, locks and unlocks facilities as needed Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Educational and/or Experience High school graduate (diploma/GED) preferred Knowledge of proper use and disposal of hazardous chemicals and products Knowledge of bodily fluid cleanup and proper protection Knowledge of all floor types, such as wood, vinyl and ceramic tile as well as carpet fibers, weaves and backings Three years of experience in a related field preferred Human Relations Skills Ability to be flexible and prioritize work in a consistently changing environment. Establish and maintain working relationships with employees, staff, and faculty and campus visitors. Follow oral and/or written instructions. Communicate effectively in both oral and written form. Ability to work as part of a team and independently as required. Physical Demands Considerable physical activity is required. Requires heavy physical work; heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling required of objects up to 50 pounds. Physical work is primary part (more than 70%) of job. Work environment involves some exposure to hazards or physical risks, which require following basic safety precautions. Work may involve moderate exposure to unusual elements, such as extreme temperatures, dirt, dust, fumes, smoke, unpleasant odors, and/or loud noises. Work in all types of weather and temperatures both indoors (temperature variation in campus buildings) and outdoors (precipitation and various temperatures). Frequent and repetitive bending, twisting, reaching, pushing, and pulling are required. Use proper lifting (up to 15 pounds) and safety techniques while climbing ladders. Other Requirements Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs Valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University's insurance plan preferred Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission ."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=990158146": {"title": "Custodian", "description": "Custodian. Job category: Physical Plant Employee type: Support Staff. Responsibilities: Demonstrate proper and effective cleaning methods in accordance with the quality control checklist Clean, sweep, vacuum, mop, scrub and wax floors, stairs and other surfaces Clean and service lavatories and shower room facilities Use light and heavy duty cleaning equipment and attachments Report items and equipment that needs repair (doors, furniture, lights, and faucets, etc.) Follow and comply with all safety and work rules and regulations, opens and closes, locks and unlocks facilities as needed Perform other duties as assigned Qualifications: Educational and/or Experience High school graduate (diploma/GED) preferred Knowledge of proper use and disposal of hazardous chemicals and products Knowledge of bodily fluid cleanup and proper protection Knowledge of all floor types, such as wood, vinyl and ceramic tile as well as carpet fibers, weaves and backings Three years of experience in a related field preferred Human Relations Skills Ability to be flexible and prioritize work in a consistently changing environment. Establish and maintain working relationships with employees, staff, and faculty and campus visitors. Follow oral and/or written instructions. Communicate effectively in both oral and written form. Ability to work as part of a team and independently as required. Physical Demands Considerable physical activity is required. Requires heavy physical work; heavy lifting, pushing, or pulling required of objects up to 50 pounds. Physical work is primary part (more than 70%) of job. Work environment involves some exposure to hazards or physical risks, which require following basic safety precautions. Work may involve moderate exposure to unusual elements, such as extreme temperatures, dirt, dust, fumes, smoke, unpleasant odors, and/or loud noises. Work in all types of weather and temperatures both indoors (temperature variation in campus buildings) and outdoors (precipitation and various temperatures). Frequent and repetitive bending, twisting, reaching, pushing, and pulling are required. Use proper lifting (up to 15 pounds) and safety techniques while climbing ladders. Other Requirements Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs Valid motor vehicle license and be insurable under the University's insurance plan preferred Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission ."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=990130626": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy", "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy. Job category: Physical Therapy, Adjunct Employee type: Adjunct Faculty. Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy Starting date: Summer 2026 Carroll University seeks an Adjunct Instructor of Physical Therapy for the Summer 2026 semester. Course: PTH6521 Integrated Clinical Experience 1 (Pediatric Experience & Team Up!) Time/Days: 8:00a.m. - 12:30pm., Tuesday/Thursday, CGS 101/104 We seek a licensed Physical Therapist to serve in an educational role, guiding college students through applied learning experiences that bridge didactic coursework with clinical practice. This position involves mentoring students at an intermediate to entry-level stage as they develop and refine their clinical skills in patient evaluation, treatment planning, and intervention. The ideal candidate will support students in applying cognitive, psychomotor, and professional behavior competencies in real-world settings, fostering increasing independence and clinical reasoning. Emphasis is placed on promoting critical thinking with medically complex clientele, reflective practice, and professional growth as students prepare for entry-level physical therapy roles. This role involves direct supervision of students during practical experiences in collaboration with faculty and community partners and ensures alignment with current and prior academic coursework. Course Description: Participation in this clinical learning experience provides students with opportunities in a variety of settings to apply knowledge and skills learned in didactic coursework at an introductory clinical level as a student physical therapist. Students integrate patient management skills and professional behaviors from previous and current didactic course work to optimize physical function, movement, performance, health, quality of life, and well-being for patients/clients in the community. Critical thinking skills are applied as students work with community populations. Reflection on experiences promotes self-assessment and professional growth at an introductory clinical level. All practical experiences occur under the supervision of faculty in consultation with community partners. Qualifications: Graduate or Master's degree in discipline. Official transcripts due upon hire. The Program: The Doctor of Physical Therapy Program at Carroll University is accredited by the Commission on Accreditation in Physical Therapy Education (CAPTE), 1111 North Fairfax Street, Alexandria, Virginia 22314; telephone: 703-706-3245; email: accreditation@apta.org; website: capteonline.org. The College: The College of Health Sciences offers undergraduate and graduate programs through seven departments; Public Health, Athletic Training, Physician Assistant Studies, Occupational Therapy, Physical Therapy, Nursing, and Department of Movement Sciences. The mission of the College of Health Science is to educate students to be innovative professionals who collaborate to serve the health needs of their communities. The College of Health Sciences currently accounts for ~60% of University enrollment. A dynamic and growing entity, College wide foci include interprofessional education and practice, the integrated approach to health, innovative learning and healthcare technologies, community partnerships and service to medically underserved populations. To support these initiatives the University has renovated a medical arts building to house and support its service learning and Community Health Service programs and partnered with the Waukesha Free Clinic at Carroll University. The College supports the interdisciplinary nature of health science education and has received federal and private foundation grant awards to support these, as well as program specific initiatives. Carroll University: Founded in 1846, Carroll University is an independent, co-educational comprehensive university grounded in the liberal arts tradition. The Pioneer Core general education curriculum has a strong emphasis on developing intercultural awareness, including cross-cultural immersion experiences. Carroll University is located in suburban Waukesha, 16 miles west of Milwaukee and 60 miles east of Madison. The University serves approximately 3521 students. The University though intentionally ecumenical and non- sectarian in approach, is related to the Presbyterian Church, USA. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission . Application: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. As part of your application, please submit a cover letter, CV and examples of teaching effectiveness. If you have past course evaluations, please submit several that demonstrate your teaching effectiveness. In lieu of course evaluations, a letter from your current supervisor attesting to your skills in the classroom environment. Carroll University is an Equal-Opportunity Employer"}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=956606081": {"title": "Custodian", "description": "Custodian. Job category: Physical Plant Employee type: Support Staff. Carroll University is hiring for Custodian positions - Full-Time, Monday through Friday 5am-1:30pm As a Custodian , you play a vital role in maintaining a clean, safe, and healthy environment for our employees and visitors. This position performs a variety of cleaning and maintenance tasks to ensure our facilities meet the highest standards of hygiene and appearance. This important work directly impacts the well-being and productivity of everyone who uses our spaces. Carroll University offers competitive benefits, such as: 4+ weeks of vacation time Sick Time Tuition Remission for employees spouses and dependent children 16 Paid Holidays Retirement Contribution and Matches Medical/Dental/Vision Coverage Summary and Requirements: Communicate effectively in both oral and written form Work in a variety of environments and buildings Follow basic safety precautions Ability to work as part of a team and individually High School Diploma/GED preferred Valid Driver\u2019s license and ability to be insured by Carroll University Ability to lift up to 50 lbs. Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University mission. Compliance: Employees may be required to report violations of law under Title IX and the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). If this position is identified a Campus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified and provided resources for reporting. The Clery Act requires all prospective employees be notified of the availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety report. You may access the most recent report here or obtain a copy at the Department of Public Safety, at 239 North East Ave., Waukesha, WI 53186."}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=923062667": {"title": "Building Maintenance Technician I", "description": "Building Maintenance Technician I. Job category: Physical Plant- Maintenance Employee type: Support Staff. At Carroll University, we are committed to identifying and employing strategies to support our goal to be and remain a socially just organization. Rooted in the Presbyterian (U.S.A.) heritage, we respect all aspects of diversity, embrace the practice of equity, expect inclusion, and embrace belonging throughout our community. Therefore, we seek applicants who can demonstrate their understanding of justice and who can inspire our increasingly diverse student population. Performs a variety of skilled maintenance for campus facilities; ensures that facilities are available for use and consistently maintained in a safe and clean condition. Areas of responsibilities include, but are not limited to o General Carpentry o Drywall o Plaster o Painting o Cabinetry and Furniture o Flooring o Appliance repair o Basic Lock and Door Hardware o Basic Plumbing o Basic Electric o Basic HVAC o General Cleaning Practices \u00b7 Demonstrates strong customer service skills and establish, maintain, and foster positive and effective working relationships. \u00b7 General practices and operational knowledge of tools used in the maintenance and repair of building facilities including upkeep and maintenance of tools. \u00b7Occupational hazards and standard safety practices. \u00b7Principles and practices of recordkeeping and facilities maintenance software. \u00b7Identifies and reports life safety and maintenance needs to the supervisor. \u00b7Comply with University facility standards of conduct. \u00b7Accomplishing work orders through our facility maintenance software on a daily basis. \u00b7Maintain and manage records on fixed asset expenditures \u00b7Assist with project work scopes for building maintenance projects. Remove snow and ice from stairs, walks and ramps including shoveling, salting and plowing. \u00b7Perform other duties as assigned by your supervisor. Qualifications: Education and/or Experience \u00b7High School Diploma/GED required \u00b72 years of experience in general maintenance preferred Human Relations Skills \u00b7Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with diverse constituencies (internal and external) \u00b7Strong written and verbal communication skills Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, religion, ability, socio-economic background, or job type. Technology Skills \u00b7PC proficient; experience with Office 365 preferred \u00b7Understanding of Word, Excel, and Power Point \u00b7Working knowledge with maintenance management system preferred Physical Demands \u00b7Ability to stoop, kneel, reach, and inspect work sites. \u00b7Sufficient strength and dexterity to perform duties and responsibilities of the position \u00b7Job requires the ability to lift, carry, push, and pull materials and objects weighing up to 100 pounds or heavier weights with assistance and/or the use of proper equipment. Environmental Elements \u00b7Work in both indoor and outdoor environments with moderate to loud noise levels. \u00b7May work in controlled temperature conditions, cold and hot temperatures, or inclement weather conditions. \u00b7Exposure to chemicals, mechanical and electrical hazards, and hazardous physical substances and fumes. Other Requirements \u00b7Must have valid driver\u2019s license and be insurable under the university\u2019s insurance plan. \u00b7 Shift changes and/or weekend work may be required by your supervisor based on departmental and University needs \u00b7Candidates must be willing and able to support and advance the University Ethos"}}, "rows": {"https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057250434": {"school_id": 1562, "posting_url": "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057250434", "job_title_in_post": "Adjunct Instructor of Physics", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Physics", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057250433": {"school_id": 1562, "posting_url": "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057250433", "job_title_in_post": "Adjunct Instructor of Mathematics", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Mathematics", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057240201": {"school_id": 1562, "posting_url": "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057240201", "job_title_in_post": "Adjunct Instructor of Education", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Education", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057236167": {"school_id": 1562, "posting_url": "https://secure4.saashr.com/ta/6153588.careers?ShowJob=1057236167", "job_title_in_post": "Adjunct I